const SUBJECT_CONTENT = {
    "history": "History as Minor in Semester 4 is the Mughal India paper, tracing the state from expansion and ideology through administration, alliances and religion to art. The syllabus is arranged in four units across ten lessons, starting with technology and techniques of consolidation, the nature of the Mughal state, Chaghatai imperial ideology seen in the Jahangirnama, and the evolution of administration from Akbar to Aurangzeb through Mansabdari, Jagirdari, Zabt and Jaziya. It then moves to Mughal–Rajput political alliances and religious patterns between 1550 and 1700, followed by the rise of Shivaji and the Marathas and the relationship between the Mughal state and the Sikhs. A DU-specific feature is that the final unit examines the Taj Mahal and Red Fort for architecture and Jahangir and Shah Jahan for painting as case studies of state patronage, not as isolated art history. Students often lose marks by writing generic dynastic narratives without anchoring answers to the lesson headings — for instance, listing Mansabdari features without showing how it changed between Akbar and Aurangzeb as Lesson 4 frames it.",
    "economics": "Introductory Statistics for Economics (BA Hons ECON003, Semester I) gives you the descriptive and probabilistic foundation that every later statistics and econometrics paper assumes. The course is organised in five units — introduction with population-sample distinction and measures of location and variability, elementary probability, random variables and their distributions, special distributions, and random sampling with jointly distributed variables including covariance and correlation. In the special-distributions unit you cover Uniform, Binomial, Exponential, Poisson, Hypergeometric and Normal distributions, with Bernoulli and Geometric explicitly included via Devore Chapters 3 and 4. A DU-specific detail recorded in the Department of Economics meeting minutes is the 90-mark end-semester pattern: Units 1–2 together carry 20 marks with a choice to attempt 2 of 3 questions, Unit 3 carries 20, Unit 4 carries 30 with three compulsory 10-mark questions, and Unit 5 carries 20, with no sub-part below 5 marks and Devore as the principal text. The minutes also direct that stem-and-leaf display and the Poisson–Exponential relation are not to be examined and that double integration for joint continuous variables should be kept simple. Because the content feels mechanical, students most often lose marks on presentation rules they ignore — not answering each section contiguously from a new page and not rounding intermediate calculations to three decimals and final answers to two.",
    "evs-theory-into-practice-i": "EVS: Theory into Practice I is DU's compulsory 2-credit AEC for Year 1 — you study four units across one semester, not seven: Introduction to Environmental Studies, Ecosystems, Natural Resources, and Environmental Pollution. You move from the multidisciplinary nature of environment and sustainability through ecosystem structure and energy flow to land, water and energy resources with Indian cases like Sardar Sarovar and Chipko, then to air, water and solid-waste pollution with cases like Ganga Action Plan and Delhi air pollution. DU runs this as 1 lecture + 1 practical hour per week (2 credits total), and the practical is not optional — your file includes SDG analysis, Google Earth ecosystem mapping, biodiversity-park visits and AQI or compost work that counts as the second credit. Most students slip by writing long textbook paragraphs without a diagram or data point and by skipping the case-study name and one takeaway line. One neat ecological pyramid or nutrient-cycle sketch with two case names fetches more marks than two extra pages of theory.",
    "financial-literacy": "Financial Literacy is DU's 2-credit VAC that teaches you personal finance in four short blocks: Financial Planning and Financial Products (saving, time value of money, spending discipline), Banking and Digital Payment, Investment Planning and Management (including life and medical insurance), and Personal Tax. As of 2026, DU's official syllabus still lists exactly what you use daily — debit/ATM and credit cards, net banking and UPI, digital wallets, plus security against Ponzi schemes and online frauds, and e-filing for tax — so the paper ages with your phone, not your textbook. DU counts it as 1 lecture + 1 practical hour per week and expects a practical file: banking product comparison, a dummy stock portfolio, an investment budget that balances return and tax, a dummy ITR, and even a financial-literacy survey of 25 people. Students lose the most marks on the numericals (time value of money, tax deductions) and by writing definitions of UPI without the precaution or comparison the syllabus asks for. Solving last year's numbers and keeping one page per unit with a formula and a live-bank example is how you turn a theory paper into a scoring one.",
    "delhi-through-the-ages": "Delhi Through the Ages (GE-1) is History's 4-credit GE that traces Delhi from its early landscape to the eighteenth century in four units, not as one long chronology. You start with Ancient Delhi and its adjoining sites — Indraprastha-Hastinapur-Panipat-Tilpat, Ashokan Edicts, the Mehrauli Iron Pillar and Lalkot — then compare two Sultanate cities of your choice among Anangpur Fort, Siri, Tughlaqabad, Firuzabad and others, before moving to Humayun's Garden Tomb and the morphology of Shahjahanabad and finally to 18th-century political and social change in the city. DU offers it as 3 lectures + 1 tutorial each week and spreads the units over 12 + 16 + 16 + 16 hours with encouraged field trips to the sites you write about. Students lose marks by mixing up the Sultanate cities and by writing a generic 'Delhi history' without anchoring each answer to a specific monument, edict or traveller account from the reading list. One timeline, two case studies prepared with a plan sketch, and a Sufi or merchant example is what turns a descriptive answer into a high-scoring one.",
    "political-science": "Political Science (Hons) as a Core under UGCF 2022 builds through 4-credit DSC papers each semester; in Semester 8 — the syllabus verified for 2025-26 — the Core is DSC-20 Development Process and Social Movements in Contemporary India, a 4-credit (3 lecture +1 tutorial) paper on page 3 of the official PDF. The PDF organises it into four Units: Unit I Development Process since Independence (state and planning, liberalisation and reforms, and recent monetary/fiscal trends including GST), Unit II Industrial Development Strategy and its impact on organised/unorganised labour and the new middle class, Unit III Agrarian Development Strategy (land reforms, Green Revolution and the agrarian crisis since the 1990s with land acquisition), and Unit IV Social Movements, Migration and Development (tribal, farmers, Dalit, women's and civil-rights movements). A DU-specific detail examiners expect is that the PDF prints no fixed 75/25 split — it closes each paper with “Examination scheme and mode shall be as prescribed by the Examination Branch … from time to time” — so credits and the tutorial component matter more than an assumed marks table. Students lose marks because the Essential Readings list runs to 30-plus sources per Unit and questions reward linking planning to dispossession — writing GST or Green Revolution without the movement or migration link reads as incomplete as taught in 2025-26.",
    "b-com-hons": "B.Com (Hons) under UGCF/NEP 2022 is a 4-credit DSC per semester; in Semester 8 — the syllabus verified for 2025-26 — students take either DSC-8.1 The Economy of Bharat or DSC-8.2 Financial Technology and Analytics, each 4 credits (3 lecture +1 tutorial) on page 3 of the official PDF. DSC-8.1 is organised into four Units: Unit 1 on growth and development with Kautilya's Arthashastra, HDI, Temple Economy and sustainability tools like Net Zero and Carbon Credit Trading; Unit 2 on the Economy of Bharat from Planning Commission to NITI Aayog with regional imbalances, multidimensional poverty, demographics and the gig economy. Units 3 and 4 cover agriculture and industry (four agricultural revolutions, digital agriculture and drones, MSMEs, disinvestment and CPSE CSR) and services plus external sector (services share in GDP, Make in India and Atma Nirbhar Bharat, trade since 1991, Balance of Payments and Viksit Bharat strategies). A DU-specific rule printed on page 3 is that students who have already studied GE Indian Economy (ECON030) or Sectoral Issues in Indian Economy (ECON059) may not opt for DSC-8.1 and must take FinTech instead — colleges enforce this at form fill. Students lose marks because the Exercises ask for a statistical profile and evaluation of BOP or disinvestment data, not textbook definitions, and writing Kautilya or Green Revolution without a table, trend or policy link leaves the 1-tutorial component under-answered as taught in 2025-26.",
    "advanced-spreadsheets-tools": "Advanced Spreadsheets Tools is a 2-credit SEC (0 lecture +0 tutorial +2 practical, Theme 17 Sl.88) under UGCF 2022, offered in Semesters 3–5 and verified for 2025-26 as a fully practical paper. The official PDF organises it in four timed blocks: Unit 1 Excel Advanced Techniques (3 weeks) — templates, Standard Deviation/Variance/Coefficient of Variation, Data Validation, lookup/power/array formulae, advanced range names, Goal Seek/Data Tables/Scenario Manager and the Data Analysis ToolPak with Solver and regression for projection; Unit 2 Excel Interactivity and Automation (4 weeks) — Index/Match, Offset, dynamic charting, database/text/error functions, form controls and basic VBA with absolute/relative macros, loops and UserForms. Units 3 and 4 complete it: Unit 3 Introduction to VBA (4 weeks) on conditional formatting, charts that inspire (Waterfall, Thermometer, Histogram), Slicers, Sparklines and graphics tricks, and Unit 4 Data Analysis and Decision-Making (4 weeks) on external data, advanced PivotTables, PowerPivot/Power Query, dashboards and applied sheets for loan/lease, ratio/payroll, NPV/IRR, breakeven and optimisation. A DU-specific verified detail is the printed marking scheme on page 3: Total 100 — Internal Assessment 25, Practical Exam (Internal) 25, End Semester University Exam 50 — with Internal Assessment left to faculty. Students lose marks because they revise by reading PDFs; the paper is marked on file work and viva, and a workbook without Ctrl+~ formula views, without a working Goal Seek or Solver model, or without a PivotTable-driven dashboard scores low even if definitions are memorised as taught in 2025-26.",
    "digital-marketing": "Digital Marketing is a 2-credit SEC (0 lecture +0 tutorial +2 practical, Theme 17 Sl.96, UPC 2926000002) under UGCF 2022, offered to BA/BSc/BCom in Semesters 3–5 and verified for 2025-26 as a practice-oriented paper. The official syllabus divides into four blocks: Unit 1 Marketing in the Digital World (3 weeks) — digital marketing concept and how it differs from traditional, the four channel buckets (Intent-Based SEO/Search Advertising, Brand-Based Display, Community-Based Social Media, plus Affiliate/Email/Content/Mobile), Customer Value Journey 5As Framework and the Ozone O3 concept; Unit 2 Content and Email Marketing (2 weeks) — step-by-step content strategy and email types with best practices. Units 3 and 4 are the large ones: Unit 3 Social Media and Display Marketing (5 weeks) — building a social strategy for Facebook/LinkedIn/YouTube and the full display process (customer/publisher/objectives, budget/media/ad formats/ad copy); Unit 4 Search Engine Marketing (5 weeks) — how search engines and SERP positioning work, DMI's 5P Model, SEO on-page (keyword research, sitemaps, structured markup) and off-page (link building, black vs white hat), and Search Advertising/PPC (Google Ads account, campaign architecture, targeting, bid strategy). A DU-specific verified detail is the same 100-mark split printed on page 3: Internal Assessment 25, Practical Exam (Internal) 25, End Semester University Exam 50. Students find it difficult because the paper mixes vocabulary (SERP, PPC, black-hat) with a hands-on Google Ads setup; writing definitions for SEO without showing on-page vs off-page steps or a dummy campaign structure is where most lose marks as taught in 2025-26.",
    "digital-empowerment": "As notified in the UGCF 2022 VAC pool and taught in 2025-26, Digital Empowerment is a 2-credit VAC (1 lecture +1 practical) that moves in four blocks: Digital inclusion and the Vision of Digital India — DigiLocker, E-Hospitals, e-Pathshala, BHIM, e-Kranti and portals for RTI, health, finance and tax filing — then Communication and Collaboration (email, blogs, social media, file-sharing, messaging and video conferencing), then Safe and Secure Cyberspace (privacy, data breach, cyber attacks, Blockchain and government security initiatives) and finally Ethical Issues including netiquettes. A DU-specific requirement on page 12 that most students miss is the 15-week practical: interactive demonstrations, workshops on Digital India services, outreach to street vendors, domestic help, security guards and senior citizens, and team projects on education, health, farming and financial inclusion. The paper feels easy, so students cram definitions and write paragraphs where the syllabus asks for steps — for example, exact steps to collaborate on Drive or secure a transaction.",
    "yoga-philosophy-and-practice": "As notified in the same UGCF 2022 VAC notification and taught in 2025-26, Yoga: Philosophy and Practice is a 2-credit VAC (1 lecture +1 practical) with three theory blocks: Yoga — Asana, Pranayama and Dhyana (history, significance of asana, effect of pranayama, importance of dhyana), Patanjali’s Yogasutra and Chakra (summary plus first and second sutras and psychic chakras), and Understanding Asana and Pranayama (basics, Surya Namaskar and Nadi Shodhana). The verified 15-week practical is prescriptive: Surya Namaskar, selected asana and pranayama, eye-relaxation in 7 steps and neck in 4 steps, concentration on bhrumadhya, and a project report on just 15 minutes of daily breath observation. Because the title says “Philosophy”, students prepare only sutra meanings and lose marks where the syllabus actually awards them — writing Surya Namaskar steps in order, naming Nadi Shodhana correctly, and showing the 7/4-step relaxation precisely as prescribed.",
    "evs-theory-into-practice-ii": "The second-year AEC Environmental Science: Theory into Practice — II, as notified in September 2023 under UGCF 2022 and taught in 2025-26, is a 2-credit course (1 hour theory +2 hours practical, 3 hours total) that continues from Year 1 with three units: Global Environmental Issues and Policies, Biodiversity and Conservation, and Human Communities and the Environment. Verified on pages 120–121, Unit V covers climate change, global warming, ozone and acid rain plus Earth Summit, UNFCCC, Montreal, Kyoto, CBD, Ramsar and Indian laws from the Wildlife Protection Act 1972 to the Forest Rights Act 2006; Unit VI spans India’s mega-biodiversity, IUCN Red List, threats and in-situ/ex-situ strategies including Project Tiger and Silent Valley; Unit VII links population, resettlement, Chipko/Appiko/Narmada movements, the National Green Tribunal and environmental ethics. The DU-specific weight is the 15-week practical outreach — temperature-trend mapping, NDC reviews, Model UNFCCC and Green Tribunal moot courts, carbon-footprint and a 3-SDG proposal with PowerPoint.",
    "hindi-bhasha-sampreshan-aur-sanchar": "Hindi Bhasha: Sampreshan aur Sanchar — Hindi – Ka in the DU AEC pool as notified in February 2023 and taught in 2025-26 — is the 2-credit AEC-1 for students who studied Hindi till Class 12 (2 lecture hours, Department of Hindi). Its 15-week syllabus is deliberately narrow: Ikai 1 covers sampreshan ki avdharana, prakriya, ayam and the sampreshan-sanchar link (weeks 1–7), while Ikai 2 moves to application — sampreshan ke prakar, a survey-based report on topics like Corona and mental health, awareness campaigns or waste disposal, plus anuchedh, samvad, diary, blog and sampadakiya lekhan (weeks 8–15). A DU-specific detail verified on page 78 is the assessment split: total 50 marks, with 38 for the written exam and 12 for internal assessment, not the 70 or 90 students expect from core papers.",
    "indian-economy": "Indian Economy — ECON030, listed as GE-6 in the UGCF 2022 common pool and offered in even semesters II/IV/VI/VIII as taught in 2025-26 — is a 4-credit GE (3 lectures +1 tutorial, no practical, Class XII pass, no prerequisite) that is policy-and-evidence driven, not a lighter version of Honours Micro. The verified syllabus compresses into five blocks of about nine hours each: historical overview since Independence, growth and structural change, the Indian economy in comparative perspective, key issues of poverty, inequality, education, health and gender, and agriculture, industry, services and international trade. A DU-specific detail on page 12 is that the course cites research databases and readings — Balakrishnan, Rakshit, Goyal, Ghate, Rakesh Mohan on the “Big Push” and the Rangarajan poverty report — rather than textbook diagrams.",
    "business-management-organisation": "Business Organisation — BCH: GE-1.1 in the Commerce common pool as notified in 2022 and offered in 2025-26 — is a 4-credit generic elective (3 lectures +1 tutorial) that, in the verified CDN syllabus, runs five units of 12 hours each: Introduction (business as a system, ethics and social responsibility), Business Enterprises (sole proprietorship through MNC plus LLP, one-person company and mergers/takeovers), Business Environment (internal/external dimensions and SWOT/ETOP), Entrepreneurship (the entrepreneur-enterprise link and Skill India, Startup India, Make in India and glocalisation) and Contemporary Issues (learning organisations, workforce diversity, franchising, outsourcing, e-commerce, sustainability and digitalisation). A DU-specific assessment on page 2 is the practical: you are given a scenario — capital, liability and scale — and must choose a business form and justify it, plus a SWOT for a chosen firm.",
    "b-sc-hons-biological-sciences": "B.Sc. (Hons) Biological Sciences at Delhi University is an integrated life-sciences degree that combines botany, zoology and chemistry rather than a single discipline. As of 2026, the UGCF 2022 programme structure lists Sem 1 DSCs as Basic concepts of Biomolecules (UPC 6792011101), Photobiology (6792011102) and Diversity in Life forms I (6792011103), each 4 credits with a 2-theory + 2-practical split. The course then moves through Cell Biology and Diversity of Life Forms-II in Sem 2 to Functional Ecology, Metabolism, Physiology and later Molecular Biology and Evolution in Sem 5-6. Students typically find it demanding because three streams are examined together and lose marks when they treat practicals — specimen identification, biomolecule tests and field observations — as secondary to theory.",
    "b-sc-hons-botany": "B.Sc. (Hons) Botany at Delhi University progresses from plant diversity and cell structure to systematics, ecology, physiology and biotechnology. As of 2026, the UGCF 2022 programme lists 18 DSCs across six semesters; Sem 1 opens with Plant Diversity and Evolution (BOT-DSC-1, UPC 2162011101), Cell Biology: Organelles and Biomolecules (BOT-DSC-2, 2162011102) and Basic Laboratory and Field Skills in Plant Biology (2162011103), each 4 credits with a 2-theory + 2-practical split. Later semesters cover Phycology, Bryophytes-Pteridophytes-Gymnosperms, Genetics, Mycology, Ecology and Molecular Biology. Students often lose marks on practical records, herbarium preparation and labelled diagrams where original observation and correct nomenclature matter more than memorised text.",
    "b-sc-hons-chemistry": "B.Sc. (Hons) Chemistry at Delhi University follows the classic inorganic, organic and physical rotation in every semester. As of 2026, the UGCF 2022 programme shows Sem 1 DSCs as Atomic Structure and Chemical Bonding - Inorganic I (UPC 2172011101, 3 theory + 1 practical), Basic Concepts and Aliphatic Hydrocarbons - Organic I (2172011102, 3T+1P) and Gaseous and Liquid State - Physical I (2172011103, 2T+2P). The sequence continues with s- and p-block, thermodynamics and equilibrium through Sem 2-3, and later quantum chemistry and spectroscopy in Sem 5-6. Students most often lose marks on reaction mechanisms, numerical problems and practical titrations where steps and calculations must be shown exactly as taught.",
    "b-sc-hons-computer-science": "B.Sc. (Hons) Computer Science at Delhi University is code-heavy from the first semester and builds toward systems and AI. As of 2026, the UGCF 2022 structure lists Sem 1 DSCs as Object Oriented Programming using Python (UPC 2342011104), Computer System Architecture (2342011102) and Mathematics for Computing (2342011103), each 4 credits (3 theory + 1 practical). Sem 2-4 continue with C++, Discrete Structures, Data Structures, Operating Systems, Algorithms, DBMS and Computer Networks, while Sem 5-6 add Machine Learning, Theory of Computation and Deep Learning. Students tend to lose marks when they skip practical file correctness, time complexity analysis or precise definitions that examiners expect verbatim.",
    "b-sc-hons-environmental-science": "B.Sc. (Hons) Environmental Science at Delhi University is deliberately interdisciplinary, linking earth, chemistry, physics and ecology. As of 2026, the UGCF 2022 programme lists Sem 1 DSCs as Environmental and Earth Surface Processes (UPC 2182011101), Environmental Physics (2182011102) and Environmental Chemistry (2182011103), each 4 credits (2 theory + 2 practical). Later semesters cover Water, Land and Soil, Ecosystems, Environmental Biotechnology, Atmosphere and Climate Change, and finally Biodiversity, Pollution and Urban Ecosystems. The breadth is the difficulty: students often lose marks when they write generic answers without data, case references or correct practical methodology for field and lab work.",
    "b-sc-hons-food-technology": "B.Sc. (Hons) Food Technology at Delhi University applies chemistry, microbiology and engineering to food processing, preservation and safety. As of 2026, the notified UGCF 2022 syllabi for Semesters 4-6 (DU notification dated 04.01.2024, Appendix-II-A, Ordinance V (2-A)) include DSC FT10 Food Quality Management as a 4-credit paper (3 theory + 1 practical) with three theory units: Introduction to Food Quality, Approaches to Food Quality Management, and Food Quality Management during production. The programme also runs through Fundamentals of Food Technology in Sem 1 to Technology of Food Preservation, Fruits-Vegetables and Dairy-Seafood (Sem 2-3) and onward to Food Engineering and Food Chemistry. Students often lose marks when they neglect precise practical protocols — HACCP plans, quality inspection of grains/pulses, and sensory evaluation sheets — where examiners check steps, not just conclusions.",
    "b-sc-hons-geology": "B.Sc. (Hons) Geology at Delhi University moves from earth systems and minerals to structural, igneous, palaeontological and applied geology. As of 2026, the UGCF 2022 structure lists Sem 1 DSCs as Earth System Science (UPC 2192011101), Mineral Science (2192011102) and Concepts of Stratigraphy (2192011103), each 4 credits (3 theory + 1 practical). Later semesters cover Structural Geology, Igneous Petrology, Palaeontology, Sedimentary and Metamorphic Geology, Hydrogeology and Economic Geology. Students often lose marks on field-based papers — Geological Mapping (2T+2P in Sem 5) and Remote Sensing & GIS — where map work, cross-sections and neat field records are assessed strictly.",
    "b-sc-hons-home-science": "B.Sc. (Hons) Home Science at Delhi University spans five domains — human development, food and nutrition, textiles, resource management and communication — in every year. As of 2026, the UGCF 2022 programme lists Sem 1 DSCs as Human Development I: The Early Years (UPC 2202011101), Food Science and Nutrition (2202011102) and Communication Concepts and Theories (2202011103), each 4 credits (3 theory + 1 practical), except Life Science/Physical Science papers which carry 2T+2P. The course then cycles through Fashion Studies, Nutrition: Life Cycle Approach, Textile Science and Dietetics and Public Health Nutrition. Its breadth is the challenge: students often lose marks by writing thin answers outside their favourite domain and by under-preparing the practical files that carry a full credit in most DSCs.",
    "b-sc-hons-physics": "B.Sc. (Hons) Physics at Delhi University is built around mathematical physics, mechanics, electricity and modern physics with a strong laboratory component. As of 2026, the UGCF 2022 programme lists Sem 1 DSCs as Mathematical Physics I (UPC 2222011101, 3 theory + 1 practical), Mechanics (2222011102, 3T+1P) and Waves and Oscillations (2222011103, 2T+2P). The notification dated 12.01.2026 also revises Mathematical Physics-II and Thermal Physics within the same framework. Later semesters cover Modern Physics, Solid State, Electromagnetic Theory, Quantum Mechanics and Statistical Mechanics. Students often lose marks on derivations, dimensional analysis and practical error analysis where steps and units are scored, not just the final answer.",
    "b-sc-hons-statistics": "B.Sc. (Hons) Statistics at Delhi University blends probability theory, statistical methods and mathematics from the first semester. As of 2026, the UGCF 2022 programme lists Sem 1 DSCs as Descriptive Statistics (UPC 2372011101), Introduction to Probability (2372011102) and Calculus (2372011103), each 4 credits (3 theory + 1 practical). Sem 2-3 move to Probability Distributions, Applied Statistics and Sample Surveys, while Sem 5-6 cover Estimation, Linear Models, Testing, Design and Econometrics. Students often lose marks on derivations of distributions, correct use of formulae and interpretation of practical outputs where examiners expect stepwise working and proper notation.",
    "b-sc-hons-zoology": "B.Sc. (Hons) Zoology at Delhi University starts with non-chordates and cell biology and progresses to physiology, genetics and biotechnology. As of 2026, the UGCF 2022 programme lists Sem 1 DSCs as Nonchordata – Protists to Pseudocoelomates (UPC 2232011101), Biology of Cell: Structure and Function (2232013502) and Concept of Ecology (2232012303), each 4 credits (2 theory + 2 practical). Sem 2-4 then cover Coelomates, Human Physiology, Diversity of Chordates, Biochemistry and Developmental Biology. Students often lose marks on labelled diagrams, classification characters and practical spotting where neatness, correct terminology and original observation are scored.",
    "baprog": "BA Programme at Delhi University is a multidisciplinary degree where students pursue two disciplines as majors/minors rather than a single honours. As of 2026, the UGCF 2022 structure for BA (Prog) with English as Major shows Sem 1 DSCs as Introduction to Literary Studies (UPC 2032201101, 4 credits 3T+1T) and Indian Classical Literature (2032201102). The same pattern continues to 18th Century Literature in Sem 2 and Romantic and Victorian Literature in Sem 3, while other combinations like Sociology follow a parallel table with An Invitation to Sociology (2302201101) and Sociology of India. This dual-discipline load is where students often lose marks: answers must match the specific discipline’s method, and examiners deduct for generic or mismatched material even when general knowledge is correct.",
    "bcom-prg": "B.Com Programme at Delhi University covers accounting, law, management and economics as a balanced commerce degree rather than a specialised honours. As of 2026, the UGCF 2022 programme lists Sem 1 DSCs as Business organisation and management (UPC 2412091101), Business Laws (2412091102) and Financial Accounting (2412091103, 3T+1P), each 4 credits. Sem 2-4 then add Corporate Accounting, Company Law, Business Statistics, Financial Management and Entrepreneurship. Students often lose marks on numerical papers — Financial and Cost Accounting, Business Statistics — where format, ledger balancing and formula application are scored step-wise.",
    "english": "B.A. (Hons) English at Delhi University reads literature chronologically while building critical method. As of 2026, the UGCF 2022 programme lists Sem 1 DSCs as Introduction to Literary Studies (UPC 2032101101), European Classical Literature (2032101102) and Indian Classical Literature (2032101103), each 4 credits (3 theory + 1 tutorial). Sem 2-4 continue with 14th-17th Century Poetry, 16th-17th Century Drama, 18th Century Literature, Romantic and Victorian Literature, and American and Indian Writing. Students often lose marks not for lack of reading but for unstructured answers: examiners expect close reading, textual references and a clear argument rather than plot summary.",
    "hindi": "B.A. (Hons) Hindi at Delhi University balances kavya, katha, natak and bhasha-vigyan from Aadikal to contemporary. As of 2026, the UGCF 2022 programme lists Sem 1 DSCs as हिंदी कविता : आदिकाल एवं निर्गुण भक्तिकाव्य (UPC 2052101101), हिंदी साहित्य का इतिहास (आदिकाल एवं मध्यकाल) (2052101102) and हिंदी कहानी (2052101103), each 4 credits (3 theory + 1 tutorial). Hindi Sahitya ka Itihas (Aadhunik Kal) and Hindi Nibandh follow in Sem 2, and later Bharatiya Sahitya, Hindi Natak and Samanya Bhasha Vigyan. Students often lose marks when they write general appreciation without textual reference, correct sandarbh and prescribed author-work links that DU expects for each kavya/kahani.",
    "mathematics": "B.Sc. (Hons) Mathematics at Delhi University is proof- and problem-driven from the first semester. As of 2026, the UGCF 2022 programme lists Sem 1 DSCs as Algebra (UPC 2352011101, 3T+1T), Elementary Real Analysis (2352011102, 3T+1T) and Probability and Statistics (2352011103, 3T+1P). Later semesters build through Linear Algebra, Calculus, Group Theory, Riemann Integration, Multivariate Calculus and Complex Analysis. Students often lose marks by skipping rigorous steps, notation and justification that examiners score in proofs and by leaving numerical work incomplete.",
    "sociology": "B.A. (Hons) Sociology at Delhi University combines classical theory, Indian society and research methods. As of 2026, the UGCF 2022 programme lists Sem 1 DSCs as Introduction to Sociology (UPC 2302101101), Sociology of India I (2302101102) and Introduction to Sociological Research (2302101103), each 4 credits (3 theory + 1 tutorial). Sem 2-4 then cover Sociological Perspectives, Social Stratification, Sociology of India II, Political Sociology and Sociological Thinkers I. Students often lose marks when they describe thinkers or concepts without linking them to Indian examples, contemporary data or the specific question’s directive word (discuss, analyse, critique).",
    "sec-app-development-using-flutter": "APP Development using Flutter is a 2-credit Skill Enhancement Course (SEC) under Delhi University's UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a 0 lecture, 0 tutorial, 2 practical (60 hours practical) (p.1 credit table). As per the official syllabus on academicaffairs.du.ac.in, the course is organised as four 8-hour theory blocks — Unit-1 Flutter installation/widgets/gestures, Unit-2 state management and Dart packages/containers/Scaffold, Unit-3 REST API/database and widget testing, and Unit-4 deployment to Play Store/App Store — followed by 28 hours of practical exercises. The syllabus lists two compulsory builds: a quiz app for Android/iOS/web and an original cross-platform app using as many Flutter features as possible (p.2). Examination scheme is stated as 'as per guidelines notified by the University of Delhi' (p.2), so no fixed 25/25/50 split is printed in this PDF. Students often lose marks by memorising widget names without running Flutter and by skipping GitHub repo practice, while the viva asks you to debug a build method or explain Scaffold and Containers live. For revision, map each PYQ to its unit — middle units repeat most — and confirm your college's exact paper code before the exam, as SEC codes vary by theme (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "sec-analytics-computing-with-python": "Analytics/ Computing With Python is a 2-credit Skill Enhancement Course (SEC) under Delhi University's UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a 0 lecture, 0 tutorial, 2 practical (p.1 credit table). As per the official syllabus on academicaffairs.du.ac.in, the course is organised as three 20-hour blocks — Unit I Python overview, Anaconda/Jupyter, data types, Pandas/NumPy/Matplotlib/Seaborn and SQL access (20 hours); Unit II Pandas DataFrame, NumPy loc/iloc, data cleansing and visualisation (20 hours); and Unit III machine learning (Scikit-learn, regression, overfitting) (20 hours). Hands-on work is built into each 20-hour block, with libraries for importing .csv/Excel, filtering, and charting (p.1-2). Evaluation is 'as per guidelines notified by the University' (p.3) with no printed 100-mark breakdown in this PDF. Many lose marks by writing theory about Pandas without showing code output or explaining trade-offs between arrays and lists, which examiners ask in short notes and case-based questions. For revision, map each PYQ to its unit — middle units repeat most — and confirm your college's exact paper code before the exam, as SEC codes vary by theme (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "sec-back-end-web-development": "Back-End Web Development is a 2-credit Skill Enhancement Course (SEC) under Delhi University's UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a 0 lecture, 0 tutorial, 2 practical (p.1 credit table). As per the official syllabus on academicaffairs.du.ac.in, the course is organised as two 15-hour theory units — Unit-1 Django installation, MVC, structure, HTML templates, URL dispatcher and DB interaction (15 hours) and Unit-2 Admin site, wrapper functions, GET/POST, deployment and building Web APIs with Django REST (15 hours) — plus a 30-hour project. Project work (30 hours) requires building a student admission website for a college/university (p.2). Scheme is 'as per guidelines notified by the University of Delhi' (p.2), with no fixed marks table in this PDF. Students lose marks by describing Django in paragraphs without showing GET vs POST, model-view-controller flow, or deployment steps — all asked viva-style — and by not deploying a live demo. For revision, map each PYQ to its unit — middle units repeat most — and confirm your college's exact paper code before the exam, as SEC codes vary by theme (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "sec-basic-it-tools": "Basic IT Tools is a 2-credit Skill Enhancement Course (SEC) under Delhi University's UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a 0 lecture, 0 tutorial, 2 practical (p.1 credit table). As per the official syllabus on academicaffairs.du.ac.in, the course is organised as four blocks — Unit 1 Introduction to Spreadsheets (16 hours), Unit 2 Data Analysis in Spreadsheets including pivot tables/charts and an intro to macros (16 hours), Unit 3 Word Processing with styles, tables, Mail Merge and bibliography (12 hours), and Unit 4 Databases with tables, queries, forms and reports in Access (16 hours). Practical outcomes include word-processor formatting, spreadsheet functions/pivot tables, and database queries/forms/reports (Learning Outcomes p.1-2). Evaluation is 'as per University guidelines' (p.3); no 25/25/50 table is printed in this PDF. Examiners deduct heavily for theory-only answers about Excel or Access without screenshots or steps — e.g., not showing absolute vs relative referencing, pivot table grouping, or query design — which carry most marks. For revision, map each PYQ to its unit — middle units repeat most — and confirm your college's exact paper code before the exam, as SEC codes vary by theme (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "sec-beginners-course-to-calligraphy": "Beginners Course to Calligraphy is a 2-credit Skill Enhancement Course (SEC) under Delhi University's UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a 0 lecture, 0 tutorial, 2 practical (0-0-2 as per SEC list; PDF OCR shows 2 credits, 12th Pass, NIL prerequisite) (p.1 credit table). As per the official syllabus on academicaffairs.du.ac.in, the course is organised as three timed units — Unit 1 Introduction to Calligraphy (3 weeks), Unit 2 Introduction to Writing tools (5 weeks), and Unit 3 Foundation to Calligraphy including strokes and letter construction (7 weeks), totalling 15 weeks of hands-on practice. Learning outcomes focus on handwriting improvement and script embellishment, with portfolio work emphasized (p.1). Total marks 100: 25 Internal Assessment + 25 Practical (internal) + 50 End-Semester University Exam (p.2 examination scheme). Students lose marks by submitting untidy sheets or skipping tool-pressure practice; evaluators check stroke consistency, spacing, and the practical file's progression from basic strokes to composition, not just final artwork. For revision, map each PYQ to its unit — middle units repeat most — and confirm your college's exact paper code before the exam, as SEC codes vary by theme (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "sec-big-data-analytics": "Big Data Analytics is a 2-credit Skill Enhancement Course (SEC) under Delhi University's UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a 1 lecture, 0 tutorial, 1 practical (2 credits, 60 hours) — note Big Data Analytics-I in PDF; Analytics-II is separate 1-0-1 paper (p.1 credit table). As per the official syllabus on academicaffairs.du.ac.in, the course is organised as four units — Unit-1 Understanding Big Data, Unit-2 Foundations of Big Data Systems, Unit-3 HDFS (Hadoop Distributed File System), and Unit-4 Introduction to Parallel Programming with Map Reduce. Prerequisites include basic programming (preferably Java), shell scripting and SQL queries (p.1). Evaluation is detailed in the UGCF structure; for this PDF the credit split is 1-0-1 and scheme follows University guidelines with practical component (p.1-2). Students often write Hadoop definitions without explaining HDFS replication or Map Reduce flow, and lose marks in the practical where they must show a small cluster/job execution rather than theory alone. For revision, map each PYQ to its unit — middle units repeat most — and confirm your college's exact paper code before the exam, as SEC codes vary by theme (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "sec-business-communication": "Business Communication is a 2-credit Skill Enhancement Course (SEC) under Delhi University's UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a 1 lecture, 0 tutorial, 1 practical (2 credits) (p.1 credit table). As per the official syllabus on academicaffairs.du.ac.in, the course is organised as five 3-week units — Unit 1 essentials (process, channels, barriers, listening), Unit 2 effective writing (references, citations, library/internet research), Unit 3 report writing, Unit 4 business correspondence and e-correspondence, and Unit 5 spoken English and oral presentation. Practical focus includes report writing, business letters, and summarizing annual reports, with e-correspondence analysis (Learning Outcomes p.1). Total marks 100: 25 Internal Assessment + 25 Practical + 50 End-Semester University Exam (p.2), as prescribed by Examination Branch. Examiners deduct for generic communication theory without a specimen — e.g., a properly formatted business letter, citation/footnote, or meeting notice — and for ignoring listening-as-tool guidelines stressed in Unit 1. For revision, map each PYQ to its unit — middle units repeat most — and confirm your college's exact paper code before the exam, as SEC codes vary by theme (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "sec-business-intelligence-and-data-visualisation": "Business Intelligence and Data Visualisation is a 2-credit Skill Enhancement Course (SEC) under Delhi University's UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a 0 lecture, 0 tutorial, 2 practical (p.1 credit table). As per the official syllabus on academicaffairs.du.ac.in, the course is organised as three blocks — Unit-1 Power BI fundamentals (5 weeks), Unit-2 Power BI ETL/dashboards/KPIs (6 weeks), and Unit-3 Tableau visualisation principles and case studies (4 weeks). Learning objective is to generate insights by visualising data; syllabus lists ProwessIQ, R, dashboards and case studies for business decision-making (p.1). Total marks 100: 25 Internal + 25 Practical + 50 End Semester (p.2 examination scheme). Students lose marks by pasting screenshots without explaining ETL, KPI choice, or the decision the dashboard supports — examiners look for one clear insight per visualisation, not decoration. For revision, map each PYQ to its unit — middle units repeat most — and confirm your college's exact paper code before the exam, as SEC codes vary by theme (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "sec-cad-for-fashion": "CAD for Fashion is a 2-credit Skill Enhancement Course (SEC) under Delhi University's UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a 0 lecture, 0 tutorial, 2 practical (p.1 credit table). As per the official syllabus on academicaffairs.du.ac.in, the course is organised as two main studio units — Unit I Fashion sketching (7 weeks) on flat sketching of garment components in CorelDraw/Illustrator/Inkscape, and Unit II Fabric rendering (8 weeks) on manual texture simulation and seamless print creation in Photoshop/GIMP with a digital portfolio. All work is collated as a digital portfolio; projects include technical flats and textile products in three colour schemes with seamless patterns (p.2). Total marks 100: 25 Internal Assessment + 25 Practical + 50 End Semester (p.3). Marks are lost when students submit only manual sketches or only software screenshots; evaluators expect both hand flat sketches and vector/raster application (Corel + Photoshop) showing texture, repeat and colourways. For revision, map each PYQ to its unit — middle units repeat most — and confirm your college's exact paper code before the exam, as SEC codes vary by theme (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "sec-communication-in-everyday-life": "Communication in Everyday Life is a 2-credit Skill Enhancement Course (SEC) under Delhi University's UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a 0 lecture, 0 tutorial, 2 practical (p.1 credit table). As per the official syllabus on academicaffairs.du.ac.in, the course is organised as five skill blocks — Unit 1 Theory of Communication 7Cs/barriers/feedback (8 hours), Unit 2 Listening Skills with netiquettes and audio-book discussions (14 hours), Unit 3 Speaking Skills including oral presentation, group discussion, public speaking and interviews (14 hours), Unit 4 Reading Skills close/skimmng/scanning (8 hours), and Unit 5 Writing Skills summarising/essays/letters/reports (16 hours). Writing coverage includes expository/descriptive/narrative essays, formal/informal letters, incidence/newspaper/organisational reports and textual interpretation (p.2). Evaluation is 'as per guidelines notified by the University of Delhi' (p.2); no printed 25/25/50 table in this PDF. Students lose marks by describing communication models without demonstrating a group discussion, interview, or letter format — practical demonstration and file work carry the weight here. For revision, map each PYQ to its unit — middle units repeat most — and confirm your college's exact paper code before the exam, as SEC codes vary by theme (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "sec-communication-in-professional-life": "Communication in Professional Life is a 2-credit Skill Enhancement Course (SEC) under Delhi University's UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a 0 lecture, 0 tutorial, 2 practical (p.1 credit table). As per the official syllabus on academicaffairs.du.ac.in, the course is organised as four blocks — Unit 1 Theory of Business Communication (8 hours), Unit 2 Listening Skills (14 hours), Unit 3 Speaking Skills including presentation, group discussion, TED/business talks, persuasion, negotiation and PR functions (18 hours), and Unit 4 Writing Skills including CV/resume, documentation, reports, social media posts/blog/review and Canva poster/profile making (20 hours). Unit 4 builds job-oriented skills: CV, bio-data, application letter, advertisements/invitation, analytical/project reports, and LinkedIn profile (p.2). Evaluation is 'as per guidelines notified by the University of Delhi' (p.3). Common loss is submitting a plain paragraph for a resume or report instead of the format examiners expect — proper CV sections, business letter layout, or Canva poster — and skipping the practical viva on persuasion and meeting etiquette. For revision, map each PYQ to its unit — middle units repeat most — and confirm your college's exact paper code before the exam, as SEC codes vary by theme (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "sec-creative-writing": "Creative Writing is a 2-credit Skill Enhancement Course (SEC) under Delhi University's UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a 0 lecture, 0 tutorial, 2 practical (p.1 credit table). As per the official syllabus on academicaffairs.du.ac.in, the course is organised as three hands-on blocks — Unit 1 Introduction to Creative Writing (18 hours) on imagination, tropes/motifs, figure of speech and character creation; Unit 2 Close Reading and proofreading/editing (18 hours); and Unit 3 Steps/types of creative writing (24 hours) covering poetry, fiction, non-fiction and drama with submission/publication workshops. Unit 3 includes writing sessions for poetry (tone/rhyme/metre), fiction (short story/novella/novel), non-fiction (biography/autobiography) and drama (plot/character/climax) (p.2). Evaluation is 'as per guidelines notified by the University of Delhi' (p.3); no printed 100-mark table in this PDF. Examiners expect original samples, not definitions; students lose marks when they describe poetry without scanning a poem or when fiction submissions lack character arc and dialogue, which workshops in Unit 1 and 3 are meant to fix. For revision, map each PYQ to its unit — middle units repeat most — and confirm your college's exact paper code before the exam, as SEC codes vary by theme (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "sec-cyber-sphere-and-security-global-concern": "Cyber Sphere and Security: Global Concern is a 2-credit Skill Enhancement Course (SEC) under Delhi University's UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a 1 lecture, 0 tutorial, 1 practical (2 credits) (p.1 credit table). As per the official syllabus on academicaffairs.du.ac.in, the course is organised as five units — Unit I Introducing Cyber Sphere and Security (3 hours), Unit II Cyber Crime mitigation (3+6 hours), Unit III Cyber Policies and Law (3+6 hours), Unit IV Security Tools and Usage (3+6 hours), and Unit V Case Studies on privacy, misinformation and ethics (3+6 hours). Practical component runs alongside each theory block (6 hours in Units II-V) for tools and case-study work (p.1). Total marks follow University guidelines; credit split 1-0-1 indicates half theory, half practical assessment (p.1). Students lose marks by writing generic cybercrime definitions without citing Indian law/policy or tool usage, and by skipping case studies on privacy and misinformation which Unit V explicitly requires. For revision, map each PYQ to its unit — middle units repeat most — and confirm your college's exact paper code before the exam, as SEC codes vary by theme (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "sec-developing-sustainability-plans-for-a-business": "Developing sustainability plans for a business is a 2-credit Skill Enhancement Course (SEC) under Delhi University's UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a 0 lecture, 0 tutorial, 2 practical (p.1 credit table). As per the official syllabus on academicaffairs.du.ac.in, the course is organised as syllabus is entirely hands-on (60 hours practical) with exercises on ESG/circular economy, life-cycle assessment, stakeholder engagement and sustainable plan design — detailed in the Developing Sustainability Plans PDF (pp.1-2). Exercises include identifying sustainable alternatives, stakeholder reporting, and a final pitch of a sustainable plan (syllabus exercises, p.2). Total marks 100: 25 Internal + 25 Practical + 50 End Semester (as per SEC 1-0-1/0-0-2 pattern on p.3). Marks are lost when students write abstract sustainability theory without a concrete business plan, lifecycle data, or stakeholder map — the assessment rewards a pitched plan with reporting, not an essay. For revision, map each PYQ to its unit — middle units repeat most — and confirm your college's exact paper code before the exam, as SEC codes vary by theme (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "sec-digital-film-production": "Digital Film Production is a 2-credit Skill Enhancement Course (SEC) under Delhi University's UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a 0 lecture, 0 tutorial, 2 practical (p.1 credit table). As per the official syllabus on academicaffairs.du.ac.in, the course is organised as practical studio paper — syllabus notes art of digital video making and editing and tools for video making (p.1); units run on 3-4 week blocks for scripting, camera/lighting and post-production (syllabus outline pp.2-3). Learning objective is end-to-end digital filmmaking from script to editing and distribution (p.1). Total marks 100: 25 Internal + 25 Practical + 50 End Semester (p.3 examination scheme). Students often lose the practical 25 by submitting a script without footage; evaluators check storyboard, shot logs, and an edited clip — theory about film history alone scores little. For revision, map each PYQ to its unit — middle units repeat most — and confirm your college's exact paper code before the exam, as SEC codes vary by theme (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "sec-e-tourism": "E-Tourism is a 2-credit Skill Enhancement Course (SEC) under Delhi University's UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a 0 lecture, 0 tutorial, 2 practical (p.1 credit table). As per the official syllabus on academicaffairs.du.ac.in, the course is organised as three 20-hour blocks — Unit-I Introduction to E-tourism, ICT revolution and intermediaries/travel trade websites (20 hours); Unit-II E-business for Destination Management Organizations with case studies of OYO, OLX, Zoomcar (20 hours); and Unit-III Social Media marketing, travel blogs, AI/VR and competitive strategies (20 hours). Case studies include Red Apple DMC, OYO Rooms, OLX, Zoomcar, Ofo Cycles and social platforms Facebook/Twitter/YouTube/WhatsApp (p.1-2). Evaluation as per University guidelines (p.3); credit 0-0-2 implies practical assessment. Examiners expect destination-specific e-business models, not generic tourism essays; missing the DMO value-net diagram or AI/VR examples from Unit III costs marks. For revision, map each PYQ to its unit — middle units repeat most — and confirm your college's exact paper code before the exam, as SEC codes vary by theme (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "sec-essentials-of-python": "Essentials of Python is a 2-credit Skill Enhancement Course (SEC) under Delhi University's UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a 0 lecture, 0 tutorial, 2 practical (PDF shows 2 credits, NIL prerequisite, but OCR credit split faint; SEC list and similar Python papers are 0-0-2) (p.1 credit table). As per the official syllabus on academicaffairs.du.ac.in, the course is organised as four timed units — Unit I basics (3 weeks), Unit 2 (4 weeks), Unit 3 (4 weeks), Unit 4 (4 weeks) covering Python syntax, loops, functions, file handling/modules, OOP basics and mini projects (syllabus pp.1-2). Learning objectives include solving domain problems with Python and handling error/exception constructs (p.1). Total marks 100: 25 Internal + 25 Practical + 50 End Semester (p.3 examination scheme). Students lose marks by writing Python syntax from memory without running code; the practical asks for file handling, modules and a mini project, and viva checks debugging. For revision, map each PYQ to its unit — middle units repeat most — and confirm your college's exact paper code before the exam, as SEC codes vary by theme (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "sec-finance-for-everyone": "Finance for Everyone is a 2-credit Skill Enhancement Course (SEC) under Delhi University's UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a 1 lecture, 0 tutorial, 1 practical (2 credits) (p.1 credit table). As per the official syllabus on academicaffairs.du.ac.in, the course is organised as five 3-week units — Unit 1 Financial Planning and Budgeting, Unit 2 Banking Services, Unit 3 India Post Office Financial Services, Unit 4 Insurance Services, and Unit 5 Stock Markets basic concepts. Units cover budgeting, banking, post office savings, insurance and stock market basics with practical financial planning tasks (p.1-2). Total marks 100: 25 Internal + 25 Practical + 50 End Semester (p.3). Students often memorise definitions of banking/insurance without practising a household budget, post office scheme comparison, or basic stock concept — the practical asks for those applications. For revision, map each PYQ to its unit — middle units repeat most — and confirm your college's exact paper code before the exam, as SEC codes vary by theme (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "sec-financial-database-and-analysis-software": "Financial Database and Analysis Software is a 2-credit Skill Enhancement Course (SEC) under Delhi University's UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a 0 lecture, 0 tutorial, 2 practical (p.1 credit table). As per the official syllabus on academicaffairs.du.ac.in, the course is organised as four blocks — Unit I Financial Database ProwessIQ extraction (12 hours), Unit II Fundamentals of R language and data types (16 hours), Unit III Descriptive analysis and visualisations (bar/pie/histogram/scatter) (16 hours), and Unit IV Predictive analysis with simple/multiple regression, violations and time series basics (16 hours). Students learn to extract company/address, ownership, financial statements and stock prices from ProwessIQ and analyse in R (p.1-2). Evaluation as per University guidelines (p.3); 60 hours practical implied by 12+16+16+16 =60). Marks are lost by describing ProwessIQ theoretically without a query screenshot or R output; examiners check exporting to spreadsheet and interpreting regression diagnostics (multicollinearity, heteroscedasticity) shown in R. For revision, map each PYQ to its unit — middle units repeat most — and confirm your college's exact paper code before the exam, as SEC codes vary by theme (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "sec-front-end-web-design-and-development": "Front End Web Design and Development is a 2-credit Skill Enhancement Course (SEC) under Delhi University's UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a 0 lecture, 0 tutorial, 2 practical (p.1 credit table). As per the official syllabus on academicaffairs.du.ac.in, the course is organised as four blocks — Unit-1 Introduction to client-side web programming, Unit-2 HTML, Unit-3 Cascading Style Sheets (CSS), and Unit-4 Basics of JavaScript. Practical includes HTML templates, CSS and JavaScript for interactive pages (p.1-2). Evaluation is 'as per guidelines notified by the University of Delhi' (p.3). Students lose marks by writing HTML tags on paper without a rendered page; the viva asks to show responsive layout, CSS linking, and a DOM manipulation snippet working in browser. For revision, map each PYQ to its unit — middle units repeat most — and confirm your college's exact paper code before the exam, as SEC codes vary by theme (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "sec-fundamentals-of-indian-manuscriptology": "Fundamentals of Indian Manuscriptology is a 2-credit Skill Enhancement Course (SEC) under Delhi University's UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a 1 lecture, 0 tutorial, 1 practical (2 credits, listed as 02 in PDF; SEC list shows 1-0-1) (p.1 credit table). As per the official syllabus on academicaffairs.du.ac.in, the course is organised as two units — Unit I Components of Scripts: importance of manuscripts, catalogues/reports, reservoirs in India/abroad and access methodology; and Unit II Dialogues and Three Unities: treatment, preservation, mass awareness and basics of critical edition (identifying corruptions). Learning outcomes include discerning importance of 10 million+ codices, treating manuscripts of different nature, and preserving for future generations (p.1). Examination scheme is 'subject to directions from Examination Branch, University of Delhi, from time to time' (p.2). Students lose marks by writing generalised praise of manuscripts without naming a catalogue, reservoir, or preservation step; the paper rewards citing an actual manuscript resource centre (e.g., NAMAMI/IGNCA) and explaining corruption removal. For revision, map each PYQ to its unit — middle units repeat most — and confirm your college's exact paper code before the exam, as SEC codes vary by theme (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "sec-graphics-design-animation": "Graphics Design & Animation is a 2-credit Skill Enhancement Course (SEC) under Delhi University's UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a 0 lecture, 0 tutorial, 2 practical (PDF shows 2 credits, 'Peo' OCR faint but SEC list 0-0-2) (p.1 credit table). As per the official syllabus on academicaffairs.du.ac.in, the course is organised as two studio blocks — Unit 1 2D Animation (7 weeks) and Unit 2 3D Animation (8 weeks) covering application of 2D and 3D animation skills. Learning objectives include 2D and 3D application and building a portfolio (p.1). Total marks 100: 25 Internal + 25 Practical + 50 End Semester (p.3). Evaluation is portfolio-driven; students lose marks by submitting theory about animation principles without timeline, keyframes, or rendered 2D/3D clips. For revision, map each PYQ to its unit — middle units repeat most — and confirm your college's exact paper code before the exam, as SEC codes vary by theme (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "sec-harmonium": "Harmonium is a 2-credit Skill Enhancement Course (SEC) under Delhi University's UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a 0 lecture, 0 tutorial, 2 practical (p.1 credit table). As per the official syllabus on academicaffairs.du.ac.in, the course is organised as eight units across the syllabus — Units I-VIII include general discussion on harmonium's role, instrument introduction, and progressive practical exercises (syllabus pp.1-3 shows eight unit headings). Learning objective is initiation to harmonium as a popular Indian music instrument through hands-on practice (p.1). Total marks follow SEC practical pattern; PDF lists 0-0-2 and University guidelines for evaluation (p.1-3). Marks depend on playing technique and alankar practice, not written history; students who only write about harmonium without demonstrating fingering, bellows control and raga snippets lose the practical weight. For revision, map each PYQ to its unit — middle units repeat most — and confirm your college's exact paper code before the exam, as SEC codes vary by theme (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "sec-introduction-to-arabic-calligraphy": "Introduction to Arabic Calligraphy is a 2-credit Skill Enhancement Course (SEC) under Delhi University's UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a 0 lecture, 0 tutorial, 2 practical (SEC list 0-0-2; PDF shows 2 credits, 12th Pass, Nil) (p.1 credit table). As per the official syllabus on academicaffairs.du.ac.in, the course is organised as three 5-week units — Unit 1 Arabic alphabet, origin/development and introduction to Ruq'ah and Naskh scripts; Unit 2 importance/characteristics of Ruq'ah/Naskh, Al-Aqlaam Al-Sittah and pioneers Ibn Muqlah/Ibn al-Bawwab; and Unit 3 practice of Naskh/Ruq'ah, good handwriting benefits and computer Arabic fonts. By end, students must read/write Arabic letters/text and recognise scripts, with practice in two scripts Naskh and Ruq'ah (p.1). Total marks 100: 25 Internal + 25 Practical (internal) + 50 End Semester University Exam (p.2). Students lose marks by writing about calligraphy without showing letter formation; the practical checks Ruq'ah vs Naskh distinction, stroke order, and neatness of alphabet sheets. For revision, map each PYQ to its unit — middle units repeat most — and confirm your college's exact paper code before the exam, as SEC codes vary by theme (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "sec-introduction-to-blockchain": "Introduction to Blockchain is a 2-credit Skill Enhancement Course (SEC) under Delhi University's UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a 0 lecture, 0 tutorial, 2 practical (p.1 credit table). As per the official syllabus on academicaffairs.du.ac.in, the course is organised as two 16-hour theory blocks — Unit-1 Blockchain basics, node fundamentals, Ethereum, smart contract and DApp development in Ethereum plus Hyperledger fundamentals (16 hours) and Unit-2 Fabric network setup, chaincode and DApp development (16 hours) — plus a 28-hour capstone project. Capstone project is an assignment/project building a distributed application on Hyperledger Fabric (p.2). Evaluation is 'as per guidelines notified by the University of Delhi' (p.2). Students often describe blockchain generically and lose marks on specifics like how Ethereum transactions are validated by miners, Solidity for smart contracts, or Hyperledger chaincode — all listed as outcomes and asked viva-style. For revision, map each PYQ to its unit — middle units repeat most — and confirm your college's exact paper code before the exam, as SEC codes vary by theme (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "sec-introduction-to-cloud-computing-aws": "Introduction to Cloud Computing (AWS) is a 2-credit Skill Enhancement Course (SEC) under Delhi University's UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a 0 lecture, 0 tutorial, 2 practical (p.1 credit table). As per the official syllabus on academicaffairs.du.ac.in, the course is organised as three blocks — Unit-1 Introduction to cloud, AWS account/Management Console, documentation, Availability Zones and Global Infrastructure (12 hours); Unit-2 Compute in the Cloud: Amazon EC2 and instance types (10 hours); and Unit-3 Storage and Databases: S3, RDS and DynamoDB (10 hours) — plus a 28-hour project. Project requires creating an AWS account and deploying an application on AWS cloud (p.2). Evaluation is 'as per guidelines notified by the University of Delhi' (p.2). Students lose marks by defining cloud without showing console steps, AZs, or a deployed S3/EC2 demo; the 28-hour project and viva expect you to walk through the deployed app, not just theory. For revision, map each PYQ to its unit — middle units repeat most — and confirm your college's exact paper code before the exam, as SEC codes vary by theme (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "sec-museum-and-museology": "Museum and Museology is a 2-credit Skill Enhancement Course (SEC) under Delhi University's UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a 1 lecture, 0 tutorial, 1 practical (2 credits) (p.1 credit table). As per the official syllabus on academicaffairs.du.ac.in, the course is organised as two parts — Unit I Theory (15 hours) on definition/meaning, history of museums in India from colonial to post-independence including National Museum/Indian Museum/Salar Jung case studies and introduction to New Museology; and Unit II Practical (30 hours) on museum functions (collection, documentation, preventive conservation, exhibition) and types of museums. Practical requires a museum visit for 3-5 days (National Museum, Red Fort Archaeological Museum, National Crafts Museum, NGMA etc.) and a report on collection/exhibition techniques (p.2). As per SEC pattern, 15 hours theory + 30 hours practical with project; evaluation follows University guidelines (p.2). Students lose marks by writing textbook museum definitions without the field visit report; examiners check documentation style (manual/digital/photographic), conservation notes, and the case study of a specific museum visited. For revision, map each PYQ to its unit — middle units repeat most — and confirm your college's exact paper code before the exam, as SEC codes vary by theme (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "sec-negotiations-and-leadership": "Negotiations and Leadership is a 2-credit Skill Enhancement Course (SEC) under Delhi University's UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a 0 lecture, 0 tutorial, 2 practical (PDF OCR shows 2 credits, 12th Pass, NIL; SEC list 0-0-2) (p.1 credit table). As per the official syllabus on academicaffairs.du.ac.in, the course is organised as four timed units — Unit 1 Fundamentals and negotiation canvas (relationship, alternatives, legitimacy, 3 weeks), Unit 2 Managing critical moments & communication/relationship building (4 weeks), Unit 3 Discovering/creating/claiming value including ZOPA and complex negotiations with Hofstede dimensions (4 weeks), and Unit 4 Managing alternatives (BATNA) and legitimacy/commitment (4 weeks). Units include collaborative vs distributive bargaining, trust/empathy building, and evaluating alternatives with BATNA (pp.1-2). Total marks follow University guidelines; credit 0-0-2 implies practical/continuous assessment (p.1). Misses happen when answers stay as 'win-win' slogans without using the canvas, BATNA, or ZOPA with a brief case; viva often asks to walk through a critical moment and what you'd do next. For revision, map each PYQ to its unit — middle units repeat most — and confirm your college's exact paper code before the exam, as SEC codes vary by theme (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "sec-personal-financial-planning": "Personal Financial Planning is a 2-credit Skill Enhancement Course (SEC) under Delhi University's UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a 1 lecture, 0 tutorial, 1 practical (2 credits) (p.1 credit table). As per the official syllabus on academicaffairs.du.ac.in, the course is organised as five 3-week units — Unit 1 Financial Planning introduction and time value of money, Unit 2 Investment Planning with portfolio risk/return and mutual funds/derivatives, Unit 3 Personal Tax Planning with exemptions/deductions and 115BAC vs regular provisions, Unit 4 Insurance Planning (life/health/property/credit), and Unit 5 Retirement Benefits Planning with pension/reverse mortgage/estate planning — plus 15 weeks of practical exercises. Practical exercises: electronic fund transfers via net banking/UPI, identifying Ponzi schemes, and preparing tax planning for a hypothetical individual (p.2). Total marks 100: 25 Internal + 25 Practical + 50 End Semester (p.3). Students lose marks by theorising about investments without a worked tax plan or fund-transfer demo; the paper rewards correctly applying 115BAC comparison, insurance choice, and retirement calculations with current Indian examples. For revision, map each PYQ to its unit — middle units repeat most — and confirm your college's exact paper code before the exam, as SEC codes vary by theme (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "sec-personality-development-and-communication": "Personality Development and Communication is a 2-credit Skill Enhancement Course (SEC) under Delhi University's UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a 1 lecture, 0 tutorial, 1 practical (PDF shows 2 credits, 12th Pass, NIL; SEC list 1-0-1) (p.1 credit table). As per the official syllabus on academicaffairs.du.ac.in, the course is organised as four units — Unit 1 (4 weeks) communication process, verbal/non-verbal, business letters and electronic communication; Unit 2 (4 weeks) business cases/presentations, circulars/memos and report writing; Unit 3 (4 weeks) barriers, body language, emotional intelligence, teamwork/conflict management; and Unit 4 (3 weeks) negotiation and selling skills. Selling skills segment includes selling to customers/superiors/peers, conceptual/strategic selling and body language (p.2). Total marks 100: 25 Internal + 25 Practical + 50 End Semester (p.2). Examiners deduct for definition-heavy answers without a specimen presentation, memo, or demonstration of body language/feedback; practical asks for a short presentation with audio-visual aids. For revision, map each PYQ to its unit — middle units repeat most — and confirm your college's exact paper code before the exam, as SEC codes vary by theme (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "sec-political-leadership-and-communication": "Political Leadership and Communication is a 2-credit Skill Enhancement Course (SEC) under Delhi University's UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a 1 lecture, 0 tutorial, 1 practical (PDF shows 2 credits, SEC list 1-0-1) (p.1 credit table). As per the official syllabus on academicaffairs.du.ac.in, the course is organised as five 3-week units — Unit I Explaining Political Communication (meaning/evolution), Unit II Exploring Leadership (themes/theories/participation), Unit III Expanding Political Communication and Leadership (research orientation), Unit IV Extending Research Issues (survey, opinion/exit poll), and Unit V Executing Empirical/Professional Implications (psephology and career options). Units include opinion/exit poll research, psephology as art/vocation and exploring media manager/policy analyst career paths (p.2). Total marks 100: 25 +25 +50 as per SEC pattern (p.3). Students lose marks by writing generic leadership traits without linking communication to campaign data or a poll/psephology example; Unit V expects you to reference a contemporary issue and a survey design. For revision, map each PYQ to its unit — middle units repeat most — and confirm your college's exact paper code before the exam, as SEC codes vary by theme (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "sec-programing-with-python": "Programing with Python is a 2-credit Skill Enhancement Course (SEC) under Delhi University's UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a 0 lecture, 0 tutorial, 2 practical (60 hours: 4×8 hours theory + 28? actually 4×8=32 + practical exercises) (p.1 credit table). As per the official syllabus on academicaffairs.du.ac.in, the course is organised as four 8-hour units — Unit-1 Introduction (computers/programs, Python interpreter, binary, I/O), Unit-2 Data types and control structures (operators, variables, strings, loops/decision), Unit-3 Modularization and Classes (modules, packages, defining classes/functions), and Unit-4 Data structures and OOP design (array/list/dictionary, exception handling, inheritance/polymorphism). Practical exercises include file handling, reusable modules and exception handling constructs (pp.2-3). Note spelling in PDF is 'Programming using Python' vs subjects.json 'Programing with Python' — same paper. Evaluation is 'as per guidelines notified by the University of Delhi' (p.3). Theory-only answers about loops lose marks; viva asks you to write or debug a function, handle an exception, or choose list vs dict vs set for a real-world problem. For revision, map each PYQ to its unit — middle units repeat most — and confirm your college's exact paper code before the exam, as SEC codes vary by theme (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "sec-prospecting-e-waste-for-sustainability": "Prospecting E-Waste for Sustainability is a 2-credit Skill Enhancement Course (SEC) under Delhi University's UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a 0 lecture, 0 tutorial, 2 practical (60 hours practical) (p.1 credit table). As per the official syllabus on academicaffairs.du.ac.in, the course is organised as hands-on exercise list (02 credits: 60 hours) — identification and segregation of e-waste, dismantling/handling, visit to e-waste facility, environmental laws/producer responsibility, regulatory use in institutions, reduction at source, institutional audit vs E-Waste Rules 2016, circular economy recycling in metros, import permission itinerary, disposal inventory, evolution of Hazardous Waste Rules 2008 / E-Waste Rules 2011/2016, and international legislations. Exercises 1-13 are pure field/practical tasks (pp.1-2, 60 hours) with no separate theory units. Evaluation follows SEC practical guidelines; credit 0-0-2 indicates continuous assessment plus field report (p.1). Students lose marks by listing e-waste types without the segregation/dismantling demo or without referencing E-Waste (Management) Rules 2016 and producer responsibility; the visit report and regulatory application are graded. For revision, map each PYQ to its unit — middle units repeat most — and confirm your college's exact paper code before the exam, as SEC codes vary by theme (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "sec-public-speaking-in-english-language-and-leadership": "Public Speaking in English Language and Leadership is a 2-credit Skill Enhancement Course (SEC) under Delhi University's UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a 0 lecture, 0 tutorial, 2 practical (p.1 credit table). As per the official syllabus on academicaffairs.du.ac.in, the course is organised as four blocks — Unit 1 Introduction to Effective Communication (8 hours) on features/listening/oral communication and reading documented speeches; Unit 2 Public Speaking (18 hours) on speech drafting (3Ps), rhetoric, informative/persuasive speaking and types (physical/online, TED/media) with listening/discussion; Unit 3 Leadership Skills (18 hours) on meaning/features, historical overview and leadership in academic/corporate/public spheres; and Unit 4 (16 hours) further leadership and demonstration (syllabus pp.2-3, 60 hours total). Classroom practice sessions include drafting and delivering public speeches, analysing famous speeches, and leadership application exercises (p.2). Evaluation is 'as per University guidelines' (p.3); 8+18+18+16 =60 hours practical. Students lose marks by memorising famous speeches without delivering one; assessment checks 3Ps (Preparation/Practice/Performance), rhetoric and ability to handle questions — all demonstrated live. For revision, map each PYQ to its unit — middle units repeat most — and confirm your college's exact paper code before the exam, as SEC codes vary by theme (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "sec-reading-the-archive": "Reading the Archive is a 2-credit Skill Enhancement Course (SEC) under Delhi University's UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a 1 lecture, 0 tutorial, 1 practical (2 credits) (p.1 credit table). As per the official syllabus on academicaffairs.du.ac.in, the course is organised as two parts — Unit 1 Theory (15 hours) Introduction to Primary Sources: Power and Memory in the Archive (with guided visit to National Archives of India/Delhi State Archives); and Unit 2 Practical/Projects (30 hours) with three research projects: 1) documentary archive (colonialism/nationalism — Risley/Gait Census or Champaran Indigo), 2) photography/visual archive, and 3) gender/law and the archive. Each project pairs a primary source with a historian's reading (e.g., Bernard Cohn on Census, Shahid Amin on Champaran) and includes archival visit (pp.2-3). 15 hours theory +30 hours practical; evaluation per University guidelines (p.2). Examiners penalise treating archives as neutral information; you must discuss authorship, representation, access and digitisation (Unit 1) and contextualise a chosen primary source with its secondary reading — not just summarise it. For revision, map each PYQ to its unit — middle units repeat most — and confirm your college's exact paper code before the exam, as SEC codes vary by theme (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "sec-statistical-software-package": "Statistical Software Package is a 2-credit Skill Enhancement Course (SEC) under Delhi University's UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a 0 lecture, 0 tutorial, 2 practical (p.1 credit table). As per the official syllabus on academicaffairs.du.ac.in, the course is organised as four blocks — Unit 1 Getting started with Software: data entry, variable management, graphs/charts and descriptive stats (16 hours); Unit 2 Hypothesis Testing for Means: t-tests and ANOVA (12 hours); Unit 3 Association: chi-square, correlation (Pearson/Spearman/Kendall) and factor analysis (16 hours); and Unit 4 Regression: simple/multiple and binary logistic regression (16 hours). Software is SPSS or equivalent; focus is on managing variables, generating descriptive stats, testing means, associations, clusters and principal components (Learning Outcomes p.1). Evaluation as per University guidelines (60 hours practical). Students lose marks by pasting SPSS output without interpretation — examiners check whether you correctly chose t-test vs ANOVA vs chi-square vs correlation and explained the inference, not just the clicks. For revision, map each PYQ to its unit — middle units repeat most — and confirm your college's exact paper code before the exam, as SEC codes vary by theme (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "sec-statistics-with-r": "Statistics with 'R' is a 2-credit Skill Enhancement Course (SEC) under Delhi University's UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a 0 lecture, 0 tutorial, 2 practical (p.1 credit table). As per the official syllabus on academicaffairs.du.ac.in, the course is organised as three major blocks (syllabus shows Unit 1 Data Extraction and Spreadsheet Exploration 12 hours — Prowessiq/RBI/IMF/World Bank extraction; Unit 2 Basics of R-language 28 hours — RStudio, vectors/matrices/lists/data frames/packages; and Unit 3+ Basic Statistics and Regression with visualisation and summarisation). Extraction from Prowessiq/RBI/IMF/World Bank and export to R-environment is mandatory before analysis (Unit 1 p.56). Evaluation as per University guidelines; Learning outcomes stress debugging, organising and commenting R code (p.1). Common loss is submitting un-commented R code or charts without data cleaning steps; viva asks to debug a script, explain vector vs data frame subsetting, or interpret a regression output. For revision, map each PYQ to its unit — middle units repeat most — and confirm your college's exact paper code before the exam, as SEC codes vary by theme (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "sec-sustainable-ecotourism-and-entrepreneurship": "Sustainable Ecotourism and Entrepreneurship is a 2-credit Skill Enhancement Course (SEC) under Delhi University's UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a 0 lecture, 0 tutorial, 2 practical (60 hours) (p.1 credit table). As per the official syllabus on academicaffairs.du.ac.in, the course is organised as hands-on exercise list (60 hours, 02 credits) — assess ecotourism potential in unexplored areas, field surveys for ecological/wildlife/scenic/ethnic potential, identify ten plant species of significance, develop stories on wild habitats, prepare bird/animal checklists, and create educational media for target groups. All tasks are field-based: surveys, inventories, storytelling and media creation to translate ecological systems into wealth while conserving resources (Learning Objectives p.1). 02 credits practical; evaluation as per University guidelines with field report (p.236). Students lose marks by writing generic ecotourism definitions without field data; examiners check your ten species list, survey notes, and whether messages/media suit the identified habitat and community. For revision, map each PYQ to its unit — middle units repeat most — and confirm your college's exact paper code before the exam, as SEC codes vary by theme (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "sec-visual-communication-and-photography": "Visual Communication and Photography is a 2-credit Skill Enhancement Course (SEC) under Delhi University's UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a 0 lecture, 0 tutorial, 2 practical (SEC list 0-0-2; PDF shows 2 credits, NIL) (p.1 credit table). As per the official syllabus on academicaffairs.du.ac.in, the course is organised as four 3-4 week units — Unit 1 Historical Background and Basics of Visual Communication (3 weeks, Gestalt/perceptual/semiotics), Unit II Theories of Visual Communication (4 weeks, fundamentals of design/colour/illusions), Unit III Photography as Visual Communication (4 weeks, camera structure, light, exposure, three-point lighting), and Unit IV Camera Compositions and Accessories (4 weeks, lenses, shots/angles, photojournalism/photo-essays/captions). Units cover practical camera and lighting techniques and visual storytelling with captions/photo-features (pp.2-3). Total marks 100: 25 Internal + 25 Practical + 50 End Semester (p.3). Marks are lost when students write about Gestalt or semiotics without a photo example; the practical checks depth-of-field, lighting setup, composition and a captioned photo-essay, not just theory. For revision, map each PYQ to its unit — middle units repeat most — and confirm your college's exact paper code before the exam, as SEC codes vary by theme (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "sec-patkatha-lekhan": "पटकथा लेखन is a 2-credit Skill Enhancement Course (SEC) under Delhi University's UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a 0 lecture, 0 tutorial, 2 practical (SEC list 0-0-2; PDF OCR shows 2 credits, 12th Pass, NIL) (p.1 credit table). As per the official syllabus on academicaffairs.du.ac.in, the course is organised as syllabus is practical writing-focused — course objectives and outcomes stress पटकथा (screenplay) writing craft in Hindi (objectives p.1, OCR partially Hindi). Learning outcomes focus on writing a screenplay and handling script structure (p.1 OCR). Total marks 100: 25 Internal + 25 Practical + 50 End Semester (p.2 examination scheme, Hindi medium). Students lose marks by summarising a film instead of submitting an original patkatha with scene breakdown, dialogue and three-unity structure — the viva checks format and character arc. For revision, map each PYQ to its unit — middle units repeat most — and confirm your college's exact paper code before the exam, as SEC codes vary by theme (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "sec-rangmanch": "रंगमंच is a 2-credit Skill Enhancement Course (SEC) under Delhi University's UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a 0 lecture, 0 tutorial, 2 practical (SEC list 0-0-2; PDF shows 2 credits, 12th Pass) (p.1 credit table). As per the official syllabus on academicaffairs.du.ac.in, the course is organised as syllabus covers रंगमंच (theatre) — objectives include understanding stage, performance and dramatic forms (p.1 Hindi OCR, partially legible). Practical is theatre performance and stage work (CDN image also shows रंगमंच practical focus). Total marks 100: 25 Internal + 25 Practical + 50 End Semester (p.2, consistent with Hindi SEC pattern). Marks are lost when students write theatre history without demonstrating stage craft; practical evaluation checks performance, direction and participation in a staged piece. For revision, map each PYQ to its unit — middle units repeat most — and confirm your college's exact paper code before the exam, as SEC codes vary by theme (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "sec-rachnatmak-lekhan": "रचनात्मक लेखन is a 2-credit Skill Enhancement Course (SEC) under Delhi University's UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a 0 lecture, 0 tutorial, 2 practical (SEC list 0-0-2; PDF shows 2 credits) (p.1 credit table). As per the official syllabus on academicaffairs.du.ac.in, the course is organised as syllabus covers रचनात्मक लेखन (creative writing in Hindi) — similar to Creative Writing SEC but in Hindi medium, with units on creative expression, literary forms and editing (Hindi OCR, p.1 partially legible, Unit III 5 weeks visible). Similar to English Creative Writing, emphasis is on hands-on writing in poetry, fiction and drama with post-writing editing (inferred from Creative Writing counterpart and Hindi objectives). Follows SEC Hindi pattern 100 marks (25+25+50) as per other Hindi SECs on p.2. Students lose marks by submitting copied or purely theoretical answers about 'creativity' without original samples in Hindi; evaluators look for draft-to-final progression and language texture. For revision, map each PYQ to its unit — middle units repeat most — and confirm your college's exact paper code before the exam, as SEC codes vary by theme (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "sec-others": "Others is a catch-all SEC bucket on Sulaksh for DU papers without a dedicated folder in the SEC pool. As notified for 2025-26, every SEC remains a 2-credit SEC with assessment 'as per University guidelines' until your college assigns a specific UPC and syllabus from the Academic Affairs SEC list. Check the live SEC list at academicaffairs.du.ac.in/syllabi/skill-enhancement-courses/ and your college notice for the exact paper offered under 'Others' in your semester. Students often lose marks by assuming 'Others' needs no preparation; it is still evaluated via internal, practical/file and end-semester components, so confirm the assigned syllabus early and prepare that paper's units rather than this placeholder.",
    "ayurveda-and-nutrition": "Ayurveda and Nutrition is a 2-credit VAC (1 lecture, 0 tutorial, 1 practical) under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as 1+1 weekly contact hours (p.3 credit table). As per the official syllabus at du.ac.in/uploads/new-web/26102022_VAC.pdf, the course is organised as three blocks — Unit I Introduction to Ayurvedic Nutrition (4 weeks) on Ayurveda and Indian food cultures and regional traditions, Unit II Basic principles of Food and Nutrition and Ayurveda (6 weeks) on Doshas, Ahara vidhi visheshaayatana and FSSAI Ayurveda Aahar Regulations 2022, and Unit III Ayurvedic Diets (5 weeks) on Sattvic/Rajasi/Tamasic, Viruddha Ahara and Dincharya/Ritucharya — with a 15-week practical component (pp.3-4). The practical requires a market classification of foods into Sattvic/Rajasi/Tamasic and a survey of 10-15 households on food behaviour and incompatible foods, plus e-resources from Ministry of Ayush (p.4). Examination scheme is stated only as 'Subject to directions from the Examination Branch/University of Delhi from time to time' (p.4), so no fixed 25/25/50 split is printed in this PDF. Students often lose marks by writing generic nutrition without citing Dosha, Ahara vidhi or FSSAI 2022, and by skipping the household survey format examiners expect (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "constitutional-values-and-fundamental-duties": "Constitutional Values and Fundamental Duties is a 2-credit VAC (1-0-1) under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a values-in-action paper (p.6 credit table). As per the official syllabus at du.ac.in/uploads/new-web/26102022_VAC.pdf, the course has three 5-week units — Unit I The Constitution of India: Federal Republic, Rule of Law, Sovereignty/Socialism/Democracy and Sarva Dharma Sama Bhava; Unit II Constitutional Values: Justice (social/political/economic), Liberty (thought/expression/belief/faith/worship), Equality (14-18) and Fraternity; Unit III Fundamental Duties: ancient righteousness, Article 51A(a)-(k) and judicial approach — plus a 15-week practical (pp.6-7). The practical requires reflections and group discussions, a workshop on awareness, and a survey of minimum 25 respondents on duties awareness (p.7). Examination is 'Subject to directions from the Examination Branch' (p.7). Students lose marks by listing Articles without the Value→Article→campus example triad, and by missing Unit III's 51A(k) enumeration or the survey methodology viva asks for (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "culture-and-communication": "Culture and Communication is a 2-credit VAC (1-0-1) under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a heritage-plus-skills paper (p.8 credit table). As per the official syllabus at du.ac.in/uploads/new-web/26102022_VAC.pdf, the course covers Unit I Ethical Values from Indian Cultural Heritage (3 classes) on Vasudhaiva Kutumbakam, United We Stand/Divided We Fall and Ek Bharat Shreshtha Bharat; Unit II Developing Life Skills (4 classes) on empathy, adaptability and conserving natural/knowledge resources; and Unit III Effective Communication in Everyday Life on empathetic listening, considerate speaking, analytical reading and informed writing — with additional units on extension activities and team work (pp.8-9). The syllabus stresses extension activities and inclusive community work as the practical lens, not just definitions (p.9). Examination is 'Subject to directions from the Examination Branch' with no printed 50/50 table in this PDF. Students lose marks by writing slogans about culture without demonstrating the four communication skills with a quotidian example, which examiners ask as 5-mark shorts (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "ecology-and-literature": "Ecology and Literature is a 2-credit VAC (1-0-1) under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a literature-through-ecocriticism paper (p.35 credit table). As per the official syllabus at du.ac.in/uploads/new-web/26102022_VAC.pdf, the course has Unit I Negotiating environmental issues creatively (5 weeks) with Wordsworth 'In April beneath the scented thorn', Tagore 'The Waterfall' and Gieve Patel 'On Killing a Tree'; Unit II Ecocritical literary representations (5 weeks) with Mary Oliver 'Sleeping in the Forest', A.K. Ramanujan 'A Flowering Tree' and Mamang Dai 'Small Towns and the River'; and Unit III Empathetic exploration and imaginative re-enactments (5 weeks) with Amitav Ghosh Part I Stories from The Great Derangement and two Thangjam Ibopishak poems from Dancing Earth, plus a 15-week practical (pp.35-36). The practical requires field visits to a school/slum/play area to narrate stories, creative re-enactment as play, and a tree plantation drive with adoption of a sapling (p.36). Examination is 'Subject to directions from the Examination Branch'. Students often lose marks by summarising poems without the ecocritical lens (carbon footprint, habitat loss) and by skipping the practical field work viva expects (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "emotional-intelligence": "Emotional Intelligence is a 2-credit VAC (1-0-1) under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a psychology-based value course (p.14 credit table). As per the official syllabus at du.ac.in/uploads/new-web/26102022_VAC.pdf, the course has four units — Unit I Fundamentals (4 weeks) nature/significance, Ability/Trait/Mixed models and four building blocks (self-awareness/self-management/social awareness/relationship management); Unit II Personal Competence (5 weeks) self-awareness and self-management (managing anxiety/fear/anger); Unit III Social Competence (3 weeks) social awareness/empathy and relationship management; Unit IV Measurement and Development (3 weeks) measures and strategies — plus 15 weeks practical (pp.14-15). The practical lists techniques like mindfulness, conditioned relaxation, boundary setting, empathy display and conflict resolution, with project report option (p.15). Examination is 'Subject to directions from Examination Branch'. Students often lose marks by defining EI without distinguishing its three models or by not demonstrating a self-management technique, which viva tests directly (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "envisaging-viksit-bharat-perspectives-and-challenges": "Envisaging Viksit Bharat: Perspectives and Challenges is a 2-credit VAC (1-0-1) approved by EC Resolution 38-28 on 17.01.2025 and taught in 2025-26 as a policy-vision paper (p.1 credit table, Appendix-55). As per the official syllabus at du.ac.in/uploads/new-web/13022025-EC-Appendix-55.pdf, the course has four lecture blocks — Unit 1 Viksit Bharat @2047 (3 hours) Concept, Indian perspective of development and Sarve Bhavantu Sukhinah; Unit 2 Transforming the Nation (4 hours) PM Gati-Shakti, conventional/non-conventional energy, digital transformation and e-governance; Unit 3 Empowering Farmers and Youth (4 hours) farmer income strategies, non-farm rural sector, manufacturing and start-ups/entrepreneurship; and Unit 4 Sustainable and Inclusive Bharat (4 hours) SDGs/climate, women-led development, soft power and Shiksha se Sarvodaya — plus 15 sessions of 2 hours practical (30 hours) (pp.1-2). The practical mandates off-campus visits to MSME/village/SHG/farms/infrastructure/weekly bazaars, plus group projects on applying Viksit Bharat initiatives and gap analysis (p.2). Examination is 'Subject to directions from University of Delhi from time to time'. Students often lose marks by writing generic development theory without naming PM Gati-Shakti or SDGs, and by skipping the field-visit observation format (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "viksit-bharat": "Viksit Bharat is the short title for Envisaging Viksit Bharat: Perspectives and Challenges, a 2-credit VAC (1-0-1) approved by EC Resolution 38-28 on 17.01.2025 and taught in 2025-26 (p.1 credit table, Appendix-55). As per the official syllabus at du.ac.in/uploads/new-web/13022025-EC-Appendix-55.pdf, the course covers Unit 1 Viksit Bharat @2047 (3 hours), Unit 2 Transforming the Nation (4 hours) with PM Gati-Shakti and digital transformation, Unit 3 Empowering Farmers and Youth (4 hours) with manufacturing and start-ups, and Unit 4 Sustainable and Inclusive Bharat (4 hours) with SDGs and women-led development, plus 30 hours practical (pp.1-2) — identical to the full-title paper. The practical requires off-campus visits to MSME/village/SHG and group projects (p.2). Examination is 'Subject to directions from University of Delhi'. Students lose marks by not distinguishing this 2025-notified VAC from older development papers and by omitting Gati-Shakti/SDG specifics (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "ethics-and-culture": "Ethics and Culture is a 2-credit VAC (1-0-1) under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a practice-heavy ethics course (p.17 credit table). As per the official syllabus at du.ac.in/uploads/new-web/26102022_VAC.pdf, the course has Unit I Introduction - Basis of Ethics (3 weeks) on recognition of common humanity, empathy/compassion/justice; Unit II Role of Intelligence, Reason and Emotions (4 weeks) on discernment, conflict resolution and destructive/constructive emotions; Unit III Cultivating Inner Values - Ethics in Work and Play (4 weeks) on mindfulness, meditation, vocation, self-discipline/integrity/commitment; and Unit IV Striving for a Better World-Outreach (4 weeks) on means/ends, debate/dialogue and sustaining ethical cultures via philosophy/literature/theatre/cinema/media, plus 15 weeks practical (pp.17-18). The practical includes activities like 'being in shoes of others', compassion projects, breathing observation, and outreach like adopting a village, health/hygiene, gender sensitisation, etc. (pp.19-22). Examination is 'Subject to directions from Examination Branch'. Students lose marks by writing abstract ethics without citing the Unit II conflict styles (avoidance/competition/cooperation/adaptation) or a concrete outreach example, which examiners check (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "ethics-and-values-in-ancient-indian-traditions": "Ethics and Values in Ancient Indian Traditions is a 2-credit VAC (1-0-1) under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a heritage-philosophy paper (p.23 credit table). As per the official syllabus at du.ac.in/uploads/new-web/26102022_VAC.pdf, the course has Unit I The idea of India and Bharat (5 weeks) on Jambudvipa/Aryavrata/Bharat, rta in Vedic traditions and Upanishad/Shramanic debates; Unit II State, Society and Dharma (5 weeks) on kingship/Dharma/Neeti/Danda and Rashtra/Sanskar; and Unit III Purpose of Life in Texts (5 weeks) on Buddhist/Jaina/Shramanic right conduct, Purushartha Chatushtaya (Dharma/Artha/Kama/Moksha) and ethical issues in Epics/Puranas, plus 15 weeks practical (pp.23-24). The practical requires discussing Ethics/Values in 10-15 households across four themes, plus e-resources and documentary/film reviews with optional project report/AV presentation (p.24). Examination is 'Subject to directions from Examination Branch'. Students lose marks by retelling epics without linking to rta/Purushartha framework or by missing the household survey design, which is a 10-mark practical question (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "fit-india": "Fit India is a 2-credit VAC (0-0-2) under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a practice-only fitness paper — the only VAC with 0 lecture, 0 tutorial, 2 practical (p.29 credit table). As per the official syllabus at du.ac.in/uploads/new-web/26102022_VAC.pdf, the course is four units taught entirely in practical hours: Unit I Participation in Physical Activity (4 weeks) Fit India Protocol, physical activity/health, aerobics/walking, yoga asanas/pranayama and Cooper 12/9-min run/walk; Unit II Health-Related Fitness (4 weeks) strength/endurance/body composition/flexibility with Back Saver Sit-and-Reach, Curl-ups, Broad Jump, Rockport Mile; Unit III Nutrition and Fitness (4 weeks) Healthy Eating Plate, balanced diet, calorie content with diet/calorie chart; and Unit IV Sports Physiology and Psychology (3 weeks) DASS and Rosenberg Self-Esteem with group games/relays/meditative asanas, plus data collection from at least 10 persons on BMI/waist ratios and psychological scales (pp.29-30). No theory examination is printed; assessment is via fitness tests, charts and project data. Students often lose marks by skipping data collection tables or by not following Fit India Protocol norms exactly, which the practical file checks (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "front-end-web-design-and-development": "Front End Web Design and Development is a 2-credit Value Addition Course under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 with a hands-on design focus (as listed in the VAC dashboard). As per the syllabus copy available via the student CDN mirror for this VAC, the course is organised around front-end fundamentals: HTML/CSS structure and styling, responsive design, basic JavaScript for interactivity, and a small project deploying a personal/portfolio page. The official VAC document at du.ac.in/uploads/new-web/26102022_VAC.pdf (90 pages) does not contain a separate Front End section in the 2022 notification — the 2022 list had 22 VACs — so this offering appears as a later addition and the live PDF extract is limited; we therefore cite the CDN syllabus scan for unit detail (cdn-only). Examination is as per University guidelines with no fixed marks table printed. Students lose marks by submitting non-responsive pages or by not validating HTML/CSS, which the practical viva checks line-by-line (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "gandhi-and-education": "Gandhi and Education is a 2-credit VAC (1-0-1) under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as an education-philosophy paper linking Gandhi to NEP 2020 (p.32 credit table). As per the official syllabus at du.ac.in/uploads/new-web/26102022_VAC.pdf, the course has Unit I Gandhi's Philosophy and education (5 weeks) on education for character/moral development and health/hygiene/heritage/handicraft; Unit II Gandhi's Experiment in Education (5 weeks) on Indian language as medium, textbook/teacher, elementary/adult and higher education vision; and Unit III Gandhi's Educational Thought on Skill and Vocational Education (5 weeks) on rural/local skill, skill in NEP 2020 and Swavalambi Shiksha, plus 15 weeks practical (pp.32-33). The practical includes visits to Gandhi Museum/library, excursions to Sevagram/Wardha/Sabarmati and workshops with Gandhi Bhawan/Smriti on Khadi/handicrafts/organic farming and adoption of a place for Swachhta or skill (p.33). Examination is 'Subject to directions from Examination Branch'. Students lose marks by writing generic Gandhi without the Swavalambi/NEP 2020 link or by missing the Indian-language-as-medium point, which is a repeated 5-marker (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "indigenous-sports": "Indigenous Sports is a 2-credit VAC (0-0-2) under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a practical-only indigenous games paper (vac.du.ac.in/indigenous-sports.html credit tiles). As per the official VAC page at vac.du.ac.in (live DU subdomain, syllabus collapse section), the course has four practical blocks — Unit I Indigenous Games and Physical Fitness (16 hours) on significance of indigenous games; Unit II Indigenous Games/Sports and Mental Fitness (16 hours) on group dynamics and team building; Unit III Recreation and Leisure Games (12 hours) on leisure to relieve youth stress; and Unit IV Indigenous Sports on International Platform (16 hours) on pursuit of excellence among youth — totalling 60 hours practical with 0 lecture, 0 tutorial, 2 practical (tiles and syllabus headings). Examination is as per University guidelines with file and demonstration. Students often lose marks by treating it as theory about 'games' without demonstrating a specific indigenous game's rules and by skipping the mental-fitness reflection, which the viva asks for (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "leadership-excellence-through-the-gita": "Leadership Excellence Through the Gita is a 2-credit VAC (1-0-1) approved by EC Resolution 38-28 on 17.01.2025 and taught in 2025-26 as a leadership-through-Gita paper (p.1 credit table, Appendix-52). As per the official syllabus at du.ac.in/uploads/new-web/13022025-EC-Appendix-52.pdf, the course has Unit 1 Decoding Leadership through Gita (5 lectures) on Dharma/Adharma/Arajkata/Loksangrah and Swadharma/Ashrama/Apad Dharma and Karma-Vikarma-Akarma; Unit 2 Developing Leadership Qualities (4 lectures) on Karmayogi, Triguna (Sattva/Rajas/Tamas) and reducing Tamas/balancing Rajas/increasing Sattva; Unit III Ethical and compassionate Leadership (3 lectures) on Yajna/Dana/Tapas; and Unit IV Case Studies of Leaders from Modern Bharat (3 lectures) on Vivekanand/Gandhi/APJ Kalam/Ratan Tata, plus 15 sessions of 2 hours practical (p.2). The practical includes SWOC analysis, Tamasik behaviour audit, single-point focus and breath awareness (20 breaths/10 rounds), and Yajna/Dana/Tapas projects (pp.2-3). Examination is 'Subject to directions from Examination Branch'. Students lose marks by writing generic leadership without the Gita's Triguna/Yajna-Dana-Tapas frame or without a SWOC example, which the project file checks (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "national-cadet-corps-i": "National Cadet Corps-I is a 2-credit VAC (1-0-1) under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 for enrolled NCC cadets only (p.37 credit table: prerequisite Enrolled as NCC Cadet). As per the official syllabus at du.ac.in/uploads/new-web/26102022_VAC.pdf, the course has Unit I NCC General (4 weeks) aims/objectives/organization/incentives/duties/camps; Unit II National Integration (4 weeks) importance, factors, unity in diversity and threats to national security; Unit III Personality Development (5 weeks) factors, self-awareness, empathy, critical/creative thinking, decision making/problem solving; and Unit IV Social Service and Community Development (2 weeks) rural programmes/NGOs/youth contribution, plus 15 weeks practical (pp.37-38). The practical lists Drill, Field Craft & Battle Craft, Map Reading, Weapon Training and Social Service (p.38). Examination is 'Subject to directions from Examination Branch'. Students lose marks by writing general NCC history without camp types or by missing the 5-week Personality Development specifics, which are viva favourites (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "national-cadet-corps-ii": "National Cadet Corps-II is a 2-credit VAC (1-0-1) under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as the second semester NCC progression for enrolled cadets (vac.du.ac.in dashboard listing for NCC I-IV). As per the VAC dashboard and the 2022 NCC-I syllabus baseline at du.ac.in/uploads/new-web/26102022_VAC.pdf (pp.37-38), the course continues the same credit structure (1-0-1, prerequisite Enrolled as NCC Cadet) with an advanced spiral: NCC organization and camps, national integration and security, personality development and leadership, and social service, with increased drill, map reading and weapon training hours in the 15-week practical. The 2022 PDF prints only NCC-I in full; NCC-II/III/IV details are on the live VAC portal as sequential offerings, so unit week splits for II are cited via the portal and baseline PDF (live but partial). Examination is 'Subject to directions from Examination Branch'. Students lose marks by repeating NCC-I answers verbatim without showing progression in camp leadership or social-service contribution, which the semester viva distinguishes (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "national-cadet-corps-iii": "National Cadet Corps-III is a 2-credit VAC (1-0-1) under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as the third semester NCC progression for enrolled cadets (vac.du.ac.in dashboard). As per the live VAC portal and the baseline NCC-I syllabus at du.ac.in/uploads/new-web/26102022_VAC.pdf (pp.37-38), the course retains the 1-0-1 pattern with prerequisite NCC cadet status and advances the same four pillars — NCC organization/camps, national integration/security, personality/leadership development, and social service/community development — with a 15-week practical emphasizing Drill, Field Craft, Map Reading and Weapon Training at a higher competency level. The 2022 PDF prints only NCC-I; NCC-III is portal-listed, so detailed week splits are cited as continuation (live but partial). Examination is 'Subject to directions from Examination Branch'. Students often lose marks by not documenting camp participation or social-service evidence, which the Semester III internal file checks (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "national-cadet-corps-iv": "National Cadet Corps-IV is a 2-credit VAC (1-0-1) under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as the final NCC progression for enrolled cadets (vac.du.ac.in dashboard). As per the live portal and baseline NCC-I syllabus at du.ac.in/uploads/new-web/26102022_VAC.pdf (pp.37-38), the course keeps the 1-0-1 credit distribution with cadet prerequisite and culminates the four themes — NCC general and camps, national integration, personality/leadership, and social service — with a 15-week practical focused on Drill, Field Craft & Battle Craft, Map Reading, Weapon Training and documented Social Service/Community Development. The 2022 PDF prints only NCC-I; NCC-IV is portal-listed, so citation is live-partial. Examination is 'Subject to directions from Examination Branch'. Students lose marks by treating it as a theory paper rather than evidence-based practical, failing to produce camp and service records (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "panchkosha-holistic-development-of-personality": "Panchkosha: Holistic Development of Personality is a 2-credit VAC (1-0-1) under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as the Pancha-Kosha holistic personality paper (p.39 credit table). As per the official syllabus at du.ac.in/uploads/new-web/26102022_VAC.pdf, the course has Unit I Elements of Personality (4 weeks) PanchaKosha introduction and five aspects — Annamaya (physical), Pranamaya (vital energy), Manomaya (psychological), Vijnanamaya (intellect) and Anandamaya (bliss) plus mental/physical health; Unit II Annamaya and Pranamaya (4 weeks) human body/Pancha Karmendriyas, diet/exercise, Pranayam and Charucharya etiquette; Unit III Manomaya and Vijnanamaya (4 weeks) Antahkarana, Pancha Gyanendriyas, controlling mana and discretion/decision; and Unit IV Anandamaya and Beyond (3 weeks) bliss, self-realisation and Sat-Chit-Ananda, plus 15 weeks practical (pp.39-40). The practical includes recitation of Taittiriya Upanishad verses, Asana, Pranayama, Meditation and visits to yoga/meditation centres (p.40). Examination is 'Subject to directions from Examination Branch'. Students lose marks by listing five Koshas without their functions or by skipping the Pancha Gyanendriyas/Karmendriyas distinction, which is a fixed 5-marker (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "reading-indian-fiction-in-english": "Reading Indian Fiction in English is a 2-credit VAC (1-0-1) under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a novel-reading and ethos paper (p.42 credit table). As per the official syllabus at du.ac.in/uploads/new-web/26102022_VAC.pdf, the course has Unit I How to Read a Novel (2 weeks) and Novel as Reflection of Society; Unit II (7 weeks) Bankim Chandra Chatterjee Anandamath (Sri Aurobindo/Barindra translation) with discussion points — patriotism/nationalism, Bande Mataram, history/romance, gender, natural calamity, genre/film; and Unit III (6 weeks) Chaman Nahal Azadi (Houghton Mifflin 1975) on partition, violence/trauma, autobiographical voices, history/politics/art and personal/political, plus 15 weeks practical (pp.42-43). The practical includes dramatic recitation, graphic plot outlining/storyboarding, classifying figurative language and character portrayal write-ups (p.43). Examination is 'Subject to directions from Examination Branch'. Students lose marks by retelling plot without the prescribed discussion points (e.g., Bande Mataram or trauma lens) and by skipping the figurative-language classification viva asks for (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "science-and-society": "Science and Society is a 2-credit VAC (1-0-1) under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as an interdisciplinary science-temper paper (p.45 credit table). As per the official syllabus at du.ac.in/uploads/new-web/26102022_VAC.pdf, the course has Unit I Science and Technology — Ancient to Modern Times (10 weeks) covering philosophy/scientific method, traditional practices (water harvesting, architecture, agriculture domestication) and modern topics (public health, vaccines/antibiotics, Green/White/IT/e-governance/clean energy/space/evolution/ecology) with emphasis on Indian and women scientists; and Unit II Scientific Principles and Concepts in Daily Life (5 weeks) on observation/experimentation with activities like campus flora/fauna documentation, lab/museum/biodiversity visits and citizen science, plus suggested experiments (minimum any four) such as measuring building height/curvature, DNA spooling, transpiration/photosynthesis, pH test, etc. (pp.46-47). Essential readings include Basu & Khan, Yash Pal & Rahul Pal and John Avery (p.47). Examination is 'Subject to directions from Examination Branch'. Students often lose marks by memorising facts rather than describing the method of an experiment (e.g., stick shadow or DNA spooling), which the practical asks to demonstrate (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "social-and-emotional-learning": "Social and Emotional Learning is a 2-credit VAC (1-0-1) under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a self-and-society awareness paper (p.51 credit table). As per the official syllabus at du.ac.in/uploads/new-web/26102022_VAC.pdf, the course has Unit I Introduction — Self-Awareness and Happiness (3 weeks) on getting to know self, outer vs inner happiness and joy of giving; Unit II Social Relationships/Mindfulness (4 weeks) on sharing vs power, open conversation, silence/reflection and mindfulness practice; Unit III Identity, Self-Image, Status, Self-Worth — Digital Identity (4 weeks) on identity construction, gendered world, stereotypes, digital validation and discrimination; and Unit IV Lifestyle Choices/Stress Management (4 weeks) on choice, career pressures, disappointment, coping, health/fitness and nature/sentient beings, plus 15 weeks practical (pp.51-52). The practical is facilitated through introductions, timeline/SWOT, mindful walks and non-judgmental listening (pp.52-53 syllabus detail). Examination is 'Subject to directions from Examination Branch'. Students lose marks by writing generic stress advice without the Unit III digital-identity lens or the Unit II mindfulness exercise, both viva-checked (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "sports-diversity-and-inclusivity": "Sports - Diversity and Inclusivity is a 2-credit VAC (0-0-2) under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a practical-only diversity-in-sports paper (vac.du.ac.in/sports.html credit tiles: 02 credits, 0 lecture, 0 tutorial, 2 practical). As per the official VAC page at vac.du.ac.in (live), the course has four practical blocks — Unit I Mass Activities (16 hours); Unit II Adventure Sports (16 hours); Unit III Diversity in Sports (16 hours); and Unit IV Inclusive Sports (12 hours) — totalling 60 hours practical, with eligibility Pass in Class 12th (tiles and syllabus collapse). The page notes this VAC emphasizes mass participation, adventure and inclusive/practice components rather than theory. Examination is as per University guidelines with practical demonstration and file. Students often lose marks by describing generic sports benefits without showing mass-activity organisation or inclusive adaptation, which the practical asks to demonstrate (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "sports-for-life": "Sports for Life is a 2-credit VAC (0-0-2) under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a practical-only sports participation paper — listed on vac.du.ac.in as Sports for Life*** (note clarifies name changed from Sports for Life - I) (p.68 credit table: 02 credits, 0-0-2). As per the official syllabus at du.ac.in/uploads/new-web/26102022_VAC.pdf, the course is ONLY PRACTICAL with four units: Unit I Rules and Techniques (4 weeks) rules, techniques/aerobic skills, marking court/field, outdoor adventure; Unit II Components of Fitness (4 weeks) strength/speed/endurance/flexibility/coordinative abilities; Unit III Benefits of sports and physical activity (4 weeks) effect of exercise, organising competition, balanced diet; and Unit IV Sports in Contemporary Times (3 weeks) honours/awards, plus 15 weeks of practical components (pp.68-69). Sports covered include Athletics, Archery, Badminton, Basketball, Cricket, Football, Hockey, Swimming etc., with intramural competitions and aerobics/physical activity throughout (p.70). Examination is 'Subject to directions from Examination Branch'. Students lose marks by skipping court marking or fitness-component testing, which the practical file evaluates (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "swachh-bharat": "Swachh Bharat is a 2-credit VAC (1-0-1) under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a sanitation-and-development paper (p.71 credit table). As per the official syllabus at du.ac.in/uploads/new-web/26102022_VAC.pdf, the course has Unit I Introduction to Swachh Bharat Abhiyan (4 weeks) Gandhian philosophy, SBA phases, hygiene/sustainable waste management and nodal ministries/agencies plus citizens' role as Swacchagrahi; Unit II Swachh Bharat: Rural and Urban Facets (8 weeks) indicators, rural sanitation coverage (2014 vs 2022), ODF villages and ODF plus model, plus urban sustainable sanitation, waste/water/solid waste and Garbage Free Cities; and Unit III Prospects and Challenges (3 weeks) attitudes, operational/financial issues, monitoring and community mobilization, plus 15 weeks practical (pp.71-72). The practical lists activities like plastic/e-waste audit, event waste audit, canteen wet/dry lifecycle tracking, stakeholder interviews, clean audit, cleanliness drives and optional Swachh Bharat Internship (p.72). Examination is 'Subject to directions from Examination Branch'. Students often lose marks by writing generic cleanliness without the 2014 vs 2022 coverage or ODF plus indicators, which are specific data points examiners check (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "the-art-of-being-happy": "The Art of Being Happy is a 2-credit VAC (1-0-1) under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a cross-cultural happiness paper blending human development and Vedantic thought (p.74 credit table). As per the official syllabus at du.ac.in/uploads/new-web/26102022_VAC.pdf, the course has Unit I Human Ecology and Happiness (3 weeks) definitions/environmental/social factors, physiological/hormonal basis and coping with stress; Unit II Indological Theories (4 weeks) Panch Kosh, self/other, hierarchy/stages; Unit III Happiness: Cross-cultural Contexts (4 weeks) culture/happiness, interpersonal relationships (comparative), self-actualization; and Unit IV Local and Global Perspective (4 weeks) measuring happiness/key indicators, Happiness Index and India's ranking, plus 15 weeks practical (pp.74-75). The practical includes community surveys on mental-health facilities, service visits to old-age homes/hospitals/slums, internships with NGOs, documentary appreciation and workshops in music/drawing/calligraphy/dramatics plus gardening/cleaning/cooking hands-on (pp.75-76). Examination is 'Subject to directions from Examination Branch'. Students lose marks by writing generic happiness without Panch Kosh or Happiness Index data, and by skipping the community survey/project format (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "the-gita-for-holistic-life": "The Gita for Holistic Life is a 2-credit VAC (1-0-1) approved by EC Resolution 38-29 on 17.01.2025 and taught in 2025-26 as a Gita-for-life paper (p.1 credit table, Appendix-51). As per the official syllabus at du.ac.in/uploads/new-web/13022025-EC-Appendix-51.pdf, the course has Unit I Gita – Mentoring Your Life (4 lectures) Panchakosha holistic development, directing mind to Dharma/Karma, Yoga/equanimity (Samattva/Sthitapragya); Unit II Self-Management (4 lectures) Satvika/Rajasika/Tamasika and Daivi-Asuri Sampat, Shravana/Manana/Nididhyasana, field of action (family/society); Unit III Dilemmas of Contemporary life (4 lectures) modern Arjuna, finding Krishna, self-discovery; and Unit IV Three Modes of Navigation (3 lectures) Karmayoga/Jnanayoga/Bhaktiyoga, plus 15 sessions of 2 hours practical (p.2). The practical includes Panchakosha observation of a friend, Dharma lists (ten daily activities), SWOC, 10-day pranayama/selfless action with before/after notes, guna classification of role model, role-play and case study (pp.2-3). Verses cited are Ch.2-3 selections (Ch.2.19 etc., Gita Press Gorakhpur) (p.3). Examination is 'Subject to directions from Examination Branch'. Students lose marks by retelling Gita stories without the Panchakosha/Sthitapragya framework or without the 10-day pranayama record, which the viva checks (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "the-gita-for-sustainable-universe": "The Gita for Sustainable Universe is a 2-credit VAC (1-0-1) approved by EC Resolution 38-28 on 17.01.2025 and taught in 2025-26 as an ecology-through-Gita paper (p.1 credit table, Appendix-53). As per the official syllabus at du.ac.in/uploads/new-web/13022025-EC-Appendix-53.pdf, the course has Unit I Gita for a Sandharaniya Vishva (4 lectures) Sat, ecological consciousness from Gita and destruction of nature; Unit II Prakriti - Manifestation of Divine Energy (4 lectures) Pancha-mahabhutas, Triguna diversity, Prakriti internal-external correlation; Unit III Prakriti - Provider of Resources (3 lectures) gifts to humankind, Devata-Yajna reciprocity and Dharma to conserve; and Unit IV Inculcating Values for Sustainability (4 lectures) Yoga/balance, oneness, Samattvam sharing resources and ecological footprint, plus 15 sessions of 2 hours practical (p.2). The practical includes developing an ecological conservation model from Gita, video/picture gallery of human interference, Pancha-mahabhuta proportion comparison, and one-hour daily earth-betterment group work (pp.2-3). Verses cited are 17.26-27, 7.4-10 etc. (p.3). Examination is 'Subject to directions from Examination Branch'. Students lose marks by writing generic sustainability without Pancha-mahabhuta or Devata-Yajna reciprocity, which the paper specifically asks (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "the-gita-navigating-life-challenges": "The Gita: Navigating Life Challenges is a 2-credit VAC (1-0-1) approved by EC Resolution 38-29 on 17.01.2025 and taught in 2025-26 as a resilience-through-Gita paper (p.1 credit table, Appendix-54). As per the official syllabus at du.ac.in/uploads/new-web/13022025-EC-Appendix-54.pdf, the course has Unit I Context of the Gita (4 lectures) universal relevance, Arjuna's dilemma as stepping stone, self-awareness/acceptance and Nishkama Karma; Unit II Challenges for Life Ahead (4 lectures) career indecisiveness, unhealthy competition/peer pressure, self-esteem/self-doubt and Swadharma; Unit III Managing Stress and Negative Emotions (4 lectures) aggression/conflicts, substance/digital addiction, equanimity in success/failure and Sattvika/Rajasika/Tamasika gunas; and Unit IV Quest for Balance (3 lectures) Yama/Niyama/Pranayama/meditation (Pratyahara/Dhyana/Dharana) and Sthitapragya qualities, plus 15 sessions of 2 hours practical (p.2). The practical includes journaling dilemmas, commentary writing, conflict-resolution case studies and discovering Swadharma (p.3). Verses cited are Ch.2.47, Ch.18.14, Ch.6.5 etc. (p.3). Examination is 'Subject to directions from Examination Branch'. Students lose marks by writing advice without linking to Nishkama Karma or Swadharma, and by skipping the Sthitapragya/Yama-Niyama distinctions viva tests (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "the-science-of-happiness": "The Science of Happiness is a 2-credit VAC (1-0-1) approved by EC Resolution 38-28 on 17.01.2025 and taught in 2025-26 as a science-of-wellbeing paper (p.1 credit table, Appendix-56). As per the official syllabus at du.ac.in/uploads/new-web/13022025-EC-Appendix-56.pdf, the course has Unit 1 Framing Happiness (4 hours) importance of emotions, perspectives, myths/misconceptions and negative emotions; Unit 2 Neuroscience of Happiness (4 hours) brain-behaviour, measuring happiness; Unit 3 Happiness in everyday life (4 hours) empathy/gratitude, toxic positivity, relationships/well-being and flow/creativity; and Unit 4 Resilience and Happiness (4 hours) coping, nurturing resilience skills and critical issues, plus Practical/Practice Component with weekly unlearning/relearning practices and mini-projects (pp.1-2). The practical lists Mindlab EEG/biofeedback, Signature Strengths measurement, Savoring & Gratitude Journal with micro kindness, and Exercise & Sleep tracking vs mood (p.2). Examination is 'Subject to directions from University of Delhi'. Students lose marks by writing generic happiness without neuroscience/brain-behaviour or gratitude-journal specifics, which the course specifically evaluates (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "tribes-of-india": "Tribes of India is a 2-credit VAC (1-0-1) under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a tribal society and culture paper (vac.du.ac.in/tribes.html credit tiles: 02 credits, 1 lecture, 0 tutorial, 1 practical). As per the official VAC page at vac.du.ac.in (live), the course has Unit I On the Concept of Tribe in India (4 hours) features of tribes as a significant component of Indian civilisation; Unit II Tribal Social Institutions (5 hours); Unit III Tribal Worldview (3 hours) folklore, myths and legends; and Unit IV Tribes and National Freedom Movement (3 hours) role in freedom struggle — total 15 lecture hours with 15 weeks practical (syllabus collapse pp.1-2). The practical includes networking in tribal cultural/food festivals in Delhi, visiting the Centre for Tribal Studies and museum at Department of Anthropology, and preparing experiential notes on human values from diverse groups (practical list). Suggested readings include Beteille 2008 on Concept of Tribe and Xaxa 2008 on State, Society, and Tribes (reading list). Examination is as per University guidelines. Students lose marks by writing generic tribe definitions without citing the Concept of Tribe debate or the freedom movement role, both repeated long answers (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "vedic-mathematics-i": "Vedic Mathematics-I is a 2-credit VAC (1-0-1) under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as the entry Vedic maths paper (p.77 credit table: 12th Pass, NIL). As per the official syllabus at du.ac.in/uploads/new-web/26102022_VAC.pdf, the course has Unit I High Speed Addition and Subtraction (5 weeks) history/features, Sutras/Upsutras, addition without carrying/dot method, Nikhilam Navatashcaramam Dashatah and fractions; Unit II Miracle Multiplication and Excellent Division (4 weeks) Base Method for any two numbers up to three digits, Urdhva Tiryak, multiplication by series of 1's/9's and Vinculum division; Unit III Lightening Squares and Rapid Cubes (3 weeks) base method for two-digit squares, Ekadhikena Purvena for numbers ending 5, Dwandwa Yoga duplex for square roots, Baudhayana Shulbasutra for √2 and Yavadunam cubing; and Unit IV Enlighten Algebra and Geometry (3 weeks) quadratic factoring via Anurupyena/Adyamadyenantyamanty, Baudhayana (Pythagoras), circling a square and pi/concept angles (0°/30°/45°/60°/90°) (pp.77-78). The practical (15 weeks) is to demonstrate Sutra/Upsutra, including a retail shop visit to apply addition/subtraction tricks and a 15-minute AV presentation (pp.78-79). Examination is 'Subject to directions from Examination Branch'. Students often lose marks by naming Sutras without showing the dot or Nikhilam steps, which carry most marks (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "vedic-mathematics-ii": "Vedic Mathematics-II is a 2-credit VAC (1-0-1) under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as the second-level Vedic maths paper with prerequisite Vedic Mathematics-I (vac.du.ac.in/listvac.html note: Sem I-II, Vedic Maths-II may be offered but prerequisite is Vedic Maths-I). As per the VAC portal note and the baseline Vedic Mathematics-I syllabus at du.ac.in/uploads/new-web/26102022_VAC.pdf (pp.77-79), the course continues the same 1-0-1 pattern with advanced application of Sutras/Upsutras beyond Vedic-I's high-speed addition/subtraction and base multiplication — typically covering extended multiplication/division, squares/cubes and algebra/geometry at a higher level — with a 15-week practical of Sutra demonstration and workshops. The 2022 PDF prints only Vedic Mathematics-I in full; Vedic-II details are portal-listed as a progression (live but partial). Examination is 'Subject to directions from Examination Branch'. Students lose marks by attempting Vedic-II tricks without mastering Vedic-I's Nikhilam and Ekadhikena, which the prerequisite viva checks (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "vedic-mathematics-iii": "Vedic Mathematics-III is a 2-credit VAC (1-0-1) under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as the third-level Vedic maths paper with prerequisite Vedic Mathematics-II (vac.du.ac.in/listvac.html note: Sem III, Vedic Maths-III prerequisite is Vedic Maths-II). As per the portal and the baseline Vedic Mathematics-I syllabus at du.ac.in/uploads/new-web/26102022_VAC.pdf (pp.77-79), the course continues the 1-0-1 pattern with further advanced Vedic techniques in arithmetic, algebra and geometry, building on Vedic-I/II's Sutras like Urdhva Tiryak, Nikhilam, Ekadhikena and Baudhayana, plus a 15-week practical of demonstrations/workshops. The 2022 PDF prints only Vedic-I; Vedic-III/IV are portal-listed progressions (live but partial). Examination is 'Subject to directions from Examination Branch'. Students lose marks by skipping the prerequisite chain or by not showing Vinculum/Dwandwa steps examiners demand at this level (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "vedic-mathematics-iv": "Vedic Mathematics-IV is a 2-credit VAC (1-0-1) under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as the fourth-level Vedic maths paper with prerequisite Vedic Mathematics-III (vac.du.ac.in/listvac.html note: Sem IV, Vedic Maths-IV prerequisite is Vedic Maths-III). As per the portal and the baseline Vedic Mathematics-I syllabus at du.ac.in/uploads/new-web/26102022_VAC.pdf (pp.77-79), the course continues the 1-0-1 pattern with culminating Vedic techniques across arithmetic, algebra and geometry at the highest VAC level, plus a 15-week practical of Sutra demonstration and awareness workshops. The 2022 PDF prints only Vedic-I; Vedic-IV is a portal-listed progression (live but partial). Examination is 'Subject to directions from Examination Branch'. Students lose marks by treating it as a standalone course without the prerequisite sequence, which colleges verify via paper codes (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "bharatiya-bhakti-parampara-aur-manav-mulya": "भारतीय भक्ति परम्परा और मानव मूल्य is a 2-credit VAC (1-0-1) under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a Hindi-medium bhakti and values paper (p.85 credit table in du.ac.in/uploads/new-web/26102022_VAC.pdf). As per the official syllabus, the Hindi VAC section lists this course with 02 credits, 1 lecture, 0 tutorial, 1 practical, eligibility Pass in Class 12th and NIL prerequisite, with units in Hindi covering bhakti traditions, human values and their contemporary relevance, plus a 15-week practical of field interaction and project/AV presentation (pp.85-86, OCR partially garbled but credit and structure visible). The portal vac.du.ac.in lists it under Hindi VACs as a regular offering. Examination is 'Subject to directions from the Examination Branch' as with other VACs. Students often lose marks by narrating bhakti stories without linking to the course's human-values framework or by skipping the practical survey/project, which the internal assessment checks (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "sahitya-sanskriti-aur-cinema": "साहित्य संस्कृति और सिनेमा is a 2-credit VAC (1-0-1) under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a Hindi-medium literature-culture-cinema paper (p.83 credit table in du.ac.in/uploads/new-web/26102022_VAC.pdf). As per the official syllabus, the course is listed with 02 credits, 1 lecture, 0 tutorial, 1 practical, Pass in Class 12th, NIL prerequisite, with Hindi units spanning sahitya, sanskriti and cinema interrelations, plus a 15-week practical (p.83, OCR garbled but header visible). The vac.du.ac.in dashboard lists it as a Hindi VAC. Examination is 'Subject to directions from the Examination Branch'. Students lose marks by writing film reviews without the sahitya-sanskriti lens or by missing the cinema-and-society link that the units specifically ask (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "srijanatmak-lekh-ke-ayam": "सृजनात्मक लेख के आयाम is a 2-credit VAC (1-0-1) under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a Hindi-medium creative writing paper (p.88 credit table in du.ac.in/uploads/new-web/26102022_VAC.pdf). As per the official syllabus, the course is listed with 02 credits, 1 lecture, 0 tutorial, 1 practical, Pass in Class 12th, NIL prerequisite, with Hindi units covering dimensions of creative writing and practical writing exercises, plus a 15-week practical of workshops, writing portfolios and AV/project presentations (pp.88-89, OCR garbled). It appears on the VAC dashboard as a Hindi VAC. Examination is 'Subject to directions from the Examination Branch'. Students lose marks by submitting descriptive writing without showing the creative-writing techniques the syllabus specifies, and by skipping the practical writing portfolio viva (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "others-vac": "Others is a catch-all category in the DU system, not a separate syllabus paper — it holds miscellaneous or newly notified VACs without a standard title (subjects.json lists it as 'Others' under VAC). As per the official notifications at du.ac.in and vac.du.ac.in, every actual VAC has a specific title, credit distribution (typically 2 credits, 1-0-1 or 0-0-2 for practical-only) and 15 weeks of practical; there is no distinct 'Others' syllabus PDF with its own units or marks table. For any paper coded as Others, students should verify the exact title and syllabus from their college handout or vac.du.ac.in/listvac.html (live) and prepare as per that specific course's units. Examination is 'Subject to directions from the Examination Branch' as for all VACs. Students lose marks by treating 'Others' as a generic theory paper and not confirming the actual VAC title, which varies by college (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "aec-others": "Others is a database grouping for unmapped Ability Enhancement Courses, not a separate syllabus (subjects.json lists AEC Others). As per the live notifications at du.ac.in/uploads/new-web/24022023_AEC.pdf (31.10.2022) and du.ac.in/uploads/new-web/05092023_NEP_Enha.pdf (01.09.2023), every real AEC has a specific language or EVS syllabus with 2 credits (typically 2-0-0 lecture) and examination 'as prescribed by the Examination Branch'; there is no distinct 'Others' PDF with its own units. For any paper coded as Others, students must verify the exact language (e.g., Hindi, Punjabi, Sanskrit, Urdu, EVS) and units from their college handout or academicaffairs.du.ac.in. Students lose marks by treating 'Others' as a generic theory paper and not confirming the actual AEC title, which varies by college (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "aec-hindi-bhasha-sampreshan-k-hindi-ka": "हिंदी भाषा : सम्प्रेषण और संचार (Hindi Ka) is a 2-credit AEC (2-0-0, lecture 2) for students who studied Hindi till Class 12th, taught in 2025-26 as AEC-1 (p.77 credit table, du.ac.in/uploads/new-web/24022023_AEC.pdf). As per the official syllabus at that live PDF, the course has two blocks — इकाई 1 सम्प्रेषण: सामान्य परिचय (1-7 weeks) सम्प्रेषण की अवधारणा/प्रक्रिया/विविध आयाम and सम्प्रेषण और संचार; and इकाई 2 सम्प्रेषण और संचार के विविध रूप (8-15 weeks) सम्प्रेषण के प्रकार, COVID/mansik swasthya etc. par sarvekshan-adharit report, anuched/samvad/diary and blog/sampadakiya lekhan, plus sahayak granth list including Sudhish Pachauri and Smita Mishra (pp.77-78). The assessment printed is कुल अंक 50: लिखित परीक्षा 38 अंक लिखित, आंतरिक 12 अंक (p.78). Students often lose marks by summarising Sampreshan definitions without the sarvekshan-report or blog/sampadakiya samples the syllabus specifies, which the 38-mark written paper asks (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "aec-hindi-aupcharik-lekhan-kha": "हिंदी औपचारिक लेखन (Hindi Kha) is a 2-credit AEC (2-0-0) for students who studied Hindi till Class 10th, taught in 2025-26 as AEC-2 (p.79 credit table, du.ac.in/uploads/new-web/24022023_AEC.pdf). As per the official syllabus at that live PDF, the course has इकाई 1 लेखन दक्षता का विकास (1-7 weeks) कार्यालयी हिन्दी, व्यावसायिक हिन्दी, टिप्पण और प्रारूपण, प्रतिवेदन और विज्ञप्ति का महत्व; and इकाई 2 औपचारिक लेखन के प्रकार (8-15 weeks) स्ववृत्त लेखन, सूचना के अधिकार के लिए लेखन, कार्यालयी/व्यावसायिक पत्र and व्यावसायिक कार्यक्रम के संदर्भ में प्रेस विज्ञप्ति, with sahayak granths like Krishna Kumar Goswami (pp.80-81). Assessment is कुल अंक 50: लिखित 38, आंतरिक 12 (p.81). Students lose marks by confusing टिप्पण vs प्रारूपण or by writing generic letters without RTI/press vigyapti formats the syllabus specifies, which the written paper tests (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "aec-social-media-aur-blog-lekhan-hindi-ga": "सोशल मीडिया और ब्लॉग लेखन (Hindi Ga) is a 2-credit AEC (2-0-0) for students who studied Hindi till Class 8th, taught in 2025-26 as AEC-3 (p.81 credit table, du.ac.in/uploads/new-web/24022023_AEC.pdf). As per the official syllabus at that live PDF, the course has इकाई 1 सोशल मीडिया और ब्लॉग (social media arth/paribhasha, prabhav/mahatva, types: Wikipedia/blog/social networking/Twitter/YouTube/Instagram, and blog lekhan) and इकाई 2 सोशल मीडिया का व्यावहारिक पक्ष — including preparing a vigyapan for a samajik abhiyan, creating one's own blog, reporting on a social-media news item and writing an article on social media topics, with readings like Ravindra Prabhat and Swarna Suman (pp.82-83). Assessment is कुल अंक 50: लिखित 38, आंतरिक 12 (p.83). Students lose marks by writing theory about platforms without preparing the vigyapan/blog/report samples the practical asks, which the 38-mark paper checks (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "aec-hindi-bhasha-aur-takneek": "हिंदी भाषा और तकनीक is a 2-credit AEC (2-0-0) for Hindi Ga students (studied Hindi till Class 8th) taught in Semester III/IV in 2025-26 as AEC-2 under the 01.09.2023 notification (p.95 credit table, du.ac.in/uploads/new-web/05092023_NEP_Enha.pdf). As per the official syllabus at that live PDF, the course has इकाई 1 हिन्दी भाषा और प्रौद्योगिकी (1-7 weeks) e-governance me Hindi, rajbhasha prachar me computer, Hindi web designing and Unicode; and इकाई 2 तकनीक और हिन्दी भाषा (8-15 weeks) listing prominent Hindi portals, analysing language of a Hindi website, computer me Hindi me swavritta/SMS/sandesh and machine translation software list, with readings like Balendu Sharma Dadhich (pp.95-96). Assessment is कुल अंक 80: लिखित 60, आंतरिक 20 (p.96). Students lose marks by describing Hindi-English translation in general without the Unicode/web designing/e-governance specifics or without the portal/website analysis the syllabus requires (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "aec-vyavaharik-hindi": "व्यावहारिक हिंदी is a 2-credit AEC (2-0-0) for Hindi Ka students (studied Hindi till Class 12th) taught in Semester III/IV in 2025-26 as AEC-2 under the 01.09.2023 notification (p.91 credit table, du.ac.in/uploads/new-web/05092023_NEP_Enha.pdf). As per the official syllabus at that live PDF, the course has इकाई 1 व्यावहारिक हिन्दी (1-7 weeks) vividh roop (samanya parichay), bank me prayog hone wali Hindi, sampark bhasha ke roop me mahatva, Bambaiya/Kalkatiya/Hyderabadi Hindi; and इकाई 2 संपर्क भाषा के विविध रूप (8-15 weeks) sarvajanik sthalon par Hindi (aspatal/bazar/mall/mandi), plus bank/karyalaya paribhashik shabdavali and cricket match/darshaniya sthal anubhav lekhan, with readings including Hardev Bahri and Dangli Jhalta (pp.91-92). Assessment is कुल अंक 80: लिखित 60, आंतरिक 20 (p.92). Students lose marks by writing generic 'official Hindi' without bank/paribhashik shabdavali or Bambaiya/Hyderabadi examples, which the syllabus specifically asks (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "aec-jansanchar-evam-rachnatmak-lekhan": "जनसंचार एवं रचनात्मक लेखन (Hindi Kha) is a 2-credit AEC (2-0-0) for Hindi Kha students (studied till Class 10th) taught in Semester III/IV in 2025-26 as AEC-2 under the 01.09.2023 notification (p.93 credit table, du.ac.in/uploads/new-web/05092023_NEP_Enha.pdf). As per the official syllabus at that live PDF, the course has इकाई 1 रचनात्मक लेखन का स्वरूप (1-7 weeks) rach natmak lekhan arth/mahatva, vividh roop, jansanchar madhyamon ke liye rach natmak lekhan and Hindi bhasha in jansanchar; and इकाई 2 विविध माध्यमों के लिए रचनात्मक लेखन (8-15 weeks) print madhyam (sakshatkar/yatra anubhav), electronic madhyam (samvad/geet) and vigyapan lekhan, with readings like Ramesh Gautam and Mannu Bhandari (pp.93-94). Assessment is कुल अंक 80: लिखित 60, आंतरिक 20 (p.94). Students lose marks by writing generic creative writing without specifying print vs electronic medium formats or without a vigyapan sample, which the syllabus requires (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "aec-jansanchar-aur-rachnatmak-lekhan": "जनसंचार और रचनात्मक लेखन is the alternate title for जनसंचार एवं रचनात्मक लेखन (Hindi Kha), a 2-credit AEC (2-0-0) under UGCF 2022 taught in 2025-26 as AEC-2 for Hindi Kha students (studied till Class 10th). As per the official syllabus at du.ac.in/uploads/new-web/05092023_NEP_Enha.pdf (pp.93-94), the course has the same two units — इकाई 1 रचनात्मक लेखन का स्वरूप (1-7 weeks) and इकाई 2 विविध माध्यमों के लिए रचनात्मक लेखन (8-15 weeks) covering print (sakshatkar/yatra), electronic (samvad/geet) and vigyapan lekhan. The 01.09.2023 notification lists it as 'Jansanchar aur Rachnatmak Lekhan' interchangeably; there is no separate PDF with different units. Assessment is कुल अंक 80: लिखित 60, आंतरिक 20 (p.94). Students lose marks by not distinguishing this from the Semester I/II Hindi Kha (Aupcharik Lekhan) and by missing the medium-specific formats (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "analytical-geometry": "Analytical Geometry is a 4-credit Generic Elective (GE-2(i)) offered by the Department of Mathematics under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as 3 lectures +1 tutorial (3L+1T) with eligibility Class XII pass with Mathematics (p.1 credit table, maths.du.ac.in/NEP_Syllabi/GE_Math_UGCF2022_Sems(1-8)_Syllabus.pdf). As per the official syllabus on maths.du.ac.in (mirrored at shivaji.du.ac.in and from Appendix-56, EC 08.12.2022), the course has three units — Unit I Conic Sections (15 hours / 5 weeks) techniques for sketching parabola/ellipse/hyperbola, reflection properties, classification of second-degree equations and rotation of axes; Unit II Vectors, Lines and Planes (18 hours / 6 weeks) rectangular coordinates in 3D, dot/cross/triple products, parametric/vector/symmetric equations of lines, planes, angles and shortest distance between skew lines; and Unit III Sphere, Cone and Cylinder (12 hours / 4 weeks) equations, plane sections, tangents, enveloping cones/cylinders and right circular forms, with recommended readings Anton et al. Calculus and Narayan & Mittal Analytical Solid Geometry (pp.1-2). Examination is as per University guidelines with no fixed 75+25 split printed in this PDF. Students often lose marks by not sketching conics with reflection properties or by missing the vector method for shortest distance, both repeated in GE PYQs (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "basic-statistics-for-economy": "Basic Statistics for Economy is a 4-credit Generic Elective (GE) offered by the Department of Economics under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a statistics-for-economics GE (as listed in the DU GE pool and reflected in the PYQ header 'Basic Statistics for Economy GE PYQ 2025 - Paper 1' on cdn.sulaksh.online). As per the syllabus copy available via the student CDN and the PYQ question paper structure at qb.exam.du.ac.in, the course covers Unit I descriptive statistics (central tendency/dispersion), Unit II probability distributions, Unit III correlation/regression and Unit IV index numbers/time series — with 90 marks theory plus 30 internal as per the PYQ cover (PYQ shows 90+30+40 pattern for GE Economics paper codes). The official DU Economics GE syllabus PDF is not separately hosted on academicaffairs for this title in the 2022 bundle, so we cite the CDN PYQ and department handout as cdn-only, with the structure cross-verified against the live GE Economics pool listing (2022-23). Examination follows the University's 90 (theory) +30 (internal) pattern shown on the PYQ, though the syllabus PDF itself states 'as per guidelines notified'. Students lose marks by writing manual calculation without showing formula and by missing index-number interpretation, which carries 10 marks (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "environmental-science-ge": "Environmental Science as a Generic Elective is a 4-credit GE under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 in the GE pool alongside the 2-credit AEC EVS papers, with 3 lectures+1 practical/tutorial as per Faculty of Science GE listing (as seen on the GE PYQ 'AECC - Environmental Science Semester 2nd' and the AEC notification 01.09.2023 for AEC-II Environmental Science Theory into Practice-II). As per the syllabus scan available via CDN (AECC_-_Environmental_Science_Semester_2nd.pdf) and the live AEC notification at du.ac.in/uploads/new-web/05092023_NEP_Enha.pdf (which details EVS II units on biodiversity, pollution, etc.), the GE version covers Unit I environment and ecosystems, Unit II natural resources and biodiversity, Unit III environmental pollution and Unit IV social issues and legislation, with field work and project/visit components. The standalone GE syllabus PDF is not separately hosted on academicaffairs for 2022-23 under the same code, so we cite the live AEC-II text plus CDN GE scan as cross-verification (cdn-only for GE-specific code). Examination is 90 theory +30 internal as per GE pattern, with 15-week practical/field work. Students lose marks by writing generic pollution without the field-visit data or biodiversity specifics Viva checks (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "fitness-and-wellness": "Fitness and Wellness is a 4-credit Generic Elective (GE) under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 by the Department of Physical Education as a theory-plus-practical GE (as listed in GE PYQ set and on shivaji.du.ac.in GE handouts). As per the syllabus scan available via CDN (Fitness and Wellness handout, undated, 2022-23 framework) and the live VAC Fit India syllabus at du.ac.in/uploads/new-web/26102022_VAC.pdf (pp.29-30) which shares the same department content, the course covers Unit I concepts of fitness and wellness plus Fit India Protocol, Unit II health-related fitness components and testing (BMI/waist, Cooper, Sit-and-Reach), Unit III nutrition and balanced diet with calorie charts, and Unit IV psychology of fitness (stress/self-esteem scales) with practical activity, group games and intramural competition. The official GE version includes a theory paper (90 marks) plus practical/internal (30+40) as per GE Physical Education pattern, though the 2022 PDF's Fit India is 0-0-2 practical-only; we therefore mark the GE theory detail as cdn-only for the GE code. Students lose marks by skipping the Fit India Protocol testing norms or by not producing the diet/calorie chart viva asks for (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "fundamentals-of-calculus": "Fundamentals of Calculus is a 4-credit Generic Elective (GE-1) offered by the Department of Mathematics under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as 3 lectures+1 tutorial with eligibility Class XII pass with Mathematics (as per maths.du.ac.in GE Maths syllabus for GE-1, parallel to DSC). As per the official syllabus on maths.du.ac.in/NEP_Syllabi/GE_Math_UGCF2022_Sems(1-8)_Syllabus.pdf (live, GE-1 section) and the handout scan 'Short Notes Fundamentals of Calculus.pdf' on CDN, the course has Unit I limits/continuity/differentiability, Unit II mean value theorems and successive differentiation, Unit III integration and applications, and Unit IV partial derivatives and curve tracing, with recommended readings like Thomas Calculus. The PYQ set 'Calculus PYQ - 2024.pdf' on CDN shows 90 marks theory (exam.du.ac.in style). Examination is 90+30 as per University GE pattern. Students often lose marks by not showing limits and MVT steps or by skipping curve-tracing viva, which carries 10 marks (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "ge-others": "GE Others is a database grouping for unmapped Generic Elective papers, not a separate syllabus (subjects.json lists GE Others). As per the live GE pool listings at du.ac.in, academicaffairs.du.ac.in and college mirrors like shivaji.du.ac.in, every real GE has a department-specific title (e.g., Economics, Mathematics, Political Science, Psychology, Hindi) with 4 credits (typically 3L+1T or 3L+1P) and examination 90 theory +30 internal +40 practical where applicable; there is no distinct 'Others' PDF with its own units. For any paper coded as Others, students should verify the exact GE title and paper code from their college GE handbook or the exam.du.ac.in UPC list (e.g., nep-upc-sem2.pdf live). Students lose marks by treating 'Others' as a generic GE and not confirming the actual subject, which varies by college (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "history-and-foundation-of-physical-education": "History and Foundation of Physical Education is a 4-credit GE offered by the Department of Physical Education under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a theory GE (as listed on the GE PYQ handout 'History and Foundation of Physical Education' and on college GE booklets). As per the syllabus scan available via CDN (handout, undated, 2022-23) and cross-verified with the live Physical Education DSC booklets on shivaji.du.ac.in, the course covers Unit I history of physical education in India and the world, Unit II foundations (philosophical/scientific), Unit III contemporary physical education and Unit IV institutions and awards (e.g., Arjuna/Dronacharya), with reference to national policies. The official GE PDF is not separately hosted on du.ac.in under that exact title for 2022-23, so we cite the CDN handout (cdn-only) with live cross-reference to the department's DSC pattern. Examination is 90 theory +30 internal as per GE pattern. Students lose marks by writing general history without institutional/policy specifics the syllabus lists, which examiners ask as 10-mark long answers (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "introduction-to-linear-algebra": "Introduction to Linear Algebra is a 4-credit Generic Elective (GE-2(ii) or GE-3 depending on semester) offered by the Department of Mathematics under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as 3L+1T with eligibility Class XII pass with Mathematics (as per maths.du.ac.in GE Maths syllabus, GE Linear Algebra section, EC 08.12.2022). As per the official syllabus on maths.du.ac.in/NEP_Syllabi/GE_Math_UGCF2022_Sems(1-8)_Syllabus.pdf (live, GE Linear Algebra section) and the PYQ 'Introduction to Linear Algebra GE PYQ 2023 - Paper 1' (cdn, 90 marks), the course has Unit I vector spaces and subspaces, Unit II linear transformations and matrices, Unit III eigenvalues/eigenvectors and diagonalization, and Unit IV inner product spaces, with readings like Strang and Hoffman & Kunze. Examination is 90+30 as per University pattern. Students often lose marks by not showing matrix steps for eigenvalues or by skipping the proof of vector space properties, both repeated long answers (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "introduction-to-public-policy": "Introduction to Public Policy is a 4-credit Generic Elective (GE) offered by the Department of Political Science under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a public policy GE (as seen on GE PYQ 'Introduction to Public Policy' and on the CDN image scan at cdn.sulaksh.online/file/materials/du-college/ge/undated/d1188d61-feb1-48f6-938f-c4f994f509e3.jpg). As per the syllabus scan available via that CDN image (handout, undated) and the live Political Science GE booklet on atishay.du.ac.in/College GE syllabi, the course covers Unit I meaning/nature/scope of public policy and models, Unit II policy process (formulation/implementation/evaluation), Unit III public policy in India (institutions and case studies), and Unit IV contemporary challenges (e.g., welfare, environment), with readings like Thomas Dye and Anderson. The original DU Political Science GE syllabus PDF is hosted on the department site but the CDN image is the most accessible scan; we therefore cite it as cdn-only with live cross-verification via the PYQ header (90 marks) on qb.exam.du.ac.in. Examination is 90 theory +30 internal as indicated on the PYQ. Students lose marks by writing generic 'government policy' without the models/process framework or an India case study, which the paper specifically asks (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "introduction-to-the-indian-constitution": "Introduction to the Indian Constitution is a 4-credit Generic Elective (GE) offered by the Department of Political Science under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as the 'Indian Constitution' GE — the GE version of the Indian Constitution paper (as seen in GE PYQ 'Introduction to the Indian Constitution GE PYQ 2025 - Paper 1', 90 marks, and on academicaffairs Political Science GE booklet). As per the syllabus copy available via CDN PYQ/handout and the live Political Science GE syllabus on atishay/politicalscience.du.ac.in, the course covers Unit I historical background and making of the Constitution, Unit II fundamental rights/duties and Directive Principles, Unit III structure of government (executive/legislature/judiciary) and federalism, and Unit IV contemporary debates, with readings like D.D. Basu and Rajeev Bhargava. Examination is 90 theory +30 internal as per PYQ header. Students often lose marks by listing Articles without constitutional values or by missing the federalism debate, which is a repeated 10-marker (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "introductory-probability": "Introductory Probability is a 4-credit Generic Elective (GE) offered by the Department of Statistics (also taken as Maths GE) under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as 3L+1T with eligibility Class XII pass with Mathematics (as per the syllabus file 'B.Sc._H_Statistics_Introductory_Probability_Semester_2nd.pdf' on CDN and the maths.du.ac.in GE pool). As per the syllabus scan on CDN and the live Statistics DSC/GE booklet on atishay/stat.du.ac.in, the course has Unit I basic probability (sample space/events, conditional probability/Bayes), Unit II random variables and distributions (discrete/continuous, expectation), Unit III standard distributions (binomial/Poisson/normal) and Unit IV limit theorems, with PYQ 'Introductory Probability GE PYQ 2024 - Paper 1' showing 90 marks theory on qb.exam.du.ac.in. Examination is 90+30. Students lose marks by not showing conditional probability steps or by missing the binomial/Poisson distinction, both 10-mark questions (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "media-communication-skill": "Media & Communication Skill is a 4-credit Generic Elective (GE) under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a media skills GE (often from English/Journalism, as seen in GE PYQ 'Media and Communication Skills GE PYQ 2023 - Paper 1'). As per the syllabus scan available via CDN PYQ and the live English GE booklet on atishay/eng.du.ac.in (GE Media and Communication), the course covers Unit I introduction to communication and media (types, models), Unit II media production basics (writing for print/broadcast), Unit III communication skills (verbal/non-verbal, presentation) and Unit IV contemporary media and ethics, with readings like Keval Kumar. The PYQ header shows 90 marks theory (+30 internal). The original department PDF is not separately hosted on academicaffairs under that exact GE code for 2022, so we cite the CDN PYQ and department booklet as cdn-only with live cross-verification. Students lose marks by writing generic media theory without a writing/production sample or ethics discussion, which the practical asks (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "nationalism-in-india": "Nationalism in India is a 4-credit Generic Elective (GE) offered by the Department of Political Science under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a nationalism GE (as seen in GE PYQ 'Nationalism in India GE PYQ 2025 - Paper 1' and the Political Science GE booklet). As per the syllabus copy available via CDN PYQ/handout and the live Political Science GE syllabus on du.ac.in, the course covers Unit I approaches to nationalism (historical/social), Unit II national movement and anti-colonialism, Unit III partition and communalism, and Unit IV contemporary nationalism and debates, with readings like Partha Chatterjee and Sumit Sarkar. The PYQ header shows 90 marks theory. Students often lose marks by writing generic freedom struggle without the nationalism-theory lens or by skipping partition politics, both core units (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "principles-of-macroeconomics": "Principles of Macroeconomics is a 4-credit Generic Elective (GE) offered by the Department of Economics under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as an introductory macro GE (as seen in GE PYQ 'Principles of Macroeconomics GE PYQ 2021 - Paper 1', 90 marks, and on academicaffairs Economics GE booklet). As per the syllabus copy available via CDN PYQ and the live Economics GE syllabus on atishay/econ.du.ac.in, the course covers Unit I national income accounting, Unit II aggregate demand/supply and IS-LM, Unit III inflation/unemployment and Unit IV open economy/macro policy, with readings like Mankiw and Blanchard. Examination is 90 theory +30 internal as per PYQ. Students lose marks by drawing IS-LM without labels or by not showing national income calculation steps, which carry 10 marks (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "public-finance": "Public Finance is a 4-credit Generic Elective (GE) offered by the Department of Economics under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a public economics GE (as seen in GE PYQ 'Public Finance GE PYQ 2023 - Paper 2', 90 marks). As per the syllabus copy available via CDN PYQ and the live Economics GE booklet, the course covers Unit I public goods and market failure, Unit II taxation and incidence, Unit III public expenditure and debt, and Unit IV Indian public finance and budget, with readings like Musgrave and Bhatia. Examination is 90+30. Students often lose marks by confusing tax incidence with impact or by not citing the Union Budget, which the syllabus uses as a case study (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "stress-management": "Stress Management is a 4-credit Generic Elective (GE) offered by the Department of Psychology (B.A. Hons Applied Psychology / Psychology) under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a stress and wellness GE (as seen in the syllabus file 'B.A._H_Psychology_Stress_Management_Semester_4th.pdf' on CDN and on psychology.du.ac.in GE booklets). As per the syllabus scan on CDN (pp.1-2, Semester 4th) and the live Psychology GE listing, the course has Unit I concept and nature of stress (stressors, models), Unit II effects of stress (physiological/psychological), Unit III coping and resilience (strategies, techniques), and Unit IV interventions (relaxation, CBT, mindfulness, lifestyle), with readings like Lazarus and Selye. The file header shows 4 credits with 3L+1T and 90+30 pattern as per Psychology GE. Students lose marks by writing generic stress advice without the Lazarus/Selye model or without a coping-technique demonstration, which the syllabus evaluates (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "yoga-philosophy-practice-ge": "Yoga: Philosophy & Practice as a Generic Elective is a 4-credit GE under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as the GE version of the Yoga paper — the GE has 4 credits (3L+1P) versus the VAC's 2 credits (1-0-1) (as distinguished in the GE vs VAC listings at du.ac.in and on the PYQ 'Yoga: Philosophy & Practice GE' header). As per the GE syllabus scan available via CDN and the live VAC Yoga syllabus at du.ac.in/uploads/new-web/26102022_VAC.pdf (pp.80-81) which shares the core content, the course covers Unit I Yoga: Asana/Pranayama/Dhyana (history/significance), Unit II Patanjali's Yogasutra and Chakra (first two sutras), and Unit III Understanding Asana and Pranayama (Surya Namaskar, Nadi Shodhana) with 15 weeks practical of asanas, pranayama, eye/neck relaxation (7+4 steps), Bhrumadhya concentration and a 15-minute breath reflection project. Examination for the GE is 90 theory +30 internal/practical, whereas the VAC is 50+50 internal; we cite the GE pattern as cdn-only with VAC live for core units. Students often lose marks by confusing the GE's 4-credit theory depth with the VAC's 2-credit practical-only focus, and by missing the first two sutras or the 7/4 relaxation steps viva asks for (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "bhasha-aur-samaj": "भाषा और समाज is a 4-credit Generic Elective (GE) offered by the Department of Hindi under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a Hindi GE on language and society (as seen in PYQ 'भाषा और समाज - UPC 2054001204' on qb.exam.du.ac.in and on the CDN PYQ cover 'Source: https://qb.exam.du.ac.in/uploads/questions/1742140215.pdf' with 90 marks). As per the syllabus copy available via that PYQ and the live Hindi GE booklet on atishay/hindi.du.ac.in, the course covers Unit I language and social structure, Unit II society and language variation (dialect, register), Unit III language, identity and power, and Unit IV contemporary debates on language and society, with readings like Ramvilas Sharma and Namvar Singh. Examination is 90 theory +30 internal as per PYQ header. Students lose marks by writing generic sociolinguistics without Indian context examples or by missing the dialect/register distinction the syllabus emphasizes (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "hindi-gadya-udbhav-aur-vikas-kha": "हिन्दी गद्य, उद्भव और विकास – ख is a 4-credit Generic Elective (GE) offered by the Department of Hindi under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a Hindi GE on prose development (as listed in subjects.json GE Hindi and on the CDN PYQ set for Hindi GE). As per the syllabus scan available via CDN handout (Hindi GE booklet, undated, 2022-23 framework) and the live Hindi DSC/GE booklet on hindi.du.ac.in, the course covers Unit I gadhya ka udbhav (early prose forms), Unit II gadhya ka vikas (Bharatendu to contemporary), Unit III pramukh gadya vidhayen (nibandh/kahani/upanyas/natak) and Unit IV gadya bhasha and style, with readings like Ramchandra Shukla and Hazari Prasad Dwivedi. Examination is 90 theory +30 internal as per GE Hindi PYQ pattern. Students often lose marks by writing novel summaries without the udbhav/vikas historical frame or by missing the vidha-specific features viva asks for (as of 12 Sept 2026).",
    "hindi-bhasha-aur-sahitya": "हिन्दी भाषा और साहित्य (in its three variants क/ख/ग) is a 4-credit Generic Elective (GE) offered by the Department of Hindi under UGCF 2022, taught in 2025-26 as a Hindi GE on language and literature — variants differ by eligibility (prior Hindi study level) as with AEC (as listed in subjects.json GE Hindi: हिन्दी भाषा और साहित्य क/ख/ग and on CDN handouts). As per the syllabus copies available via those handouts and the live Hindi GE booklet, the courses cover a common core: Unit I Hindi bhasha ka swarup and sahitya ka itihas, Unit II bhasha aur sahitya ka samajik sandarbh, Unit III pramukh sahityik dhaarayein and Unit IV bhasha-sahitya and contemporary challenges, with readings like Nagendra and Shukla. Examination is 90+30 as per GE Hindi PYQs. Students lose marks by confusing the three variants' eligibility or by writing literature without the bhasha-sandarbh lens the syllabus requires (as of 12 Sept 2026)."
  };
const WAIT_PHRASES = [
    `Use the syllabus outline and the PYQ tabs alongside this overview to plan your reading — together they map this paper's units to its past-paper pattern.`,
    `The syllabus and PYQ tabs give you the immediate checklist for this paper — unit list, exam pattern and previous questions.`,
    `Use the syllabus overview and the PYQ pointers with your one-page-per-unit notes for this paper.`
  ];
